/** worker-slot-12 — siman 473 editorial EN (332 blocks) */
export const EN = {
  "ateret-zekenim/part-001.txt|1:_": "And one does not wash hands at all, etc. But according to us, who wash all year before Kiddush, even now he should wash hands before Kiddush by rinsing his feet and not recite a berachah, and then for dipping he does not even need washing (Maharshal in responsa siman 86):",
  "ateret-zekenim/part-001.txt|5:_": "These are vegetables with which a person fulfills [the mitzvah] — eighteen endives that they call in Polish zarzowich (Maharam Isserles and Akiva b. Meir wrote that he found in a medical book that there is no connection with the herb called zarzach as Maharam Isserles wished to explain):",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|1:א": "(א) <b>Shehecheyanu.</b> And if he did not say it on day one, he blesses on day two or on one of the other days when he remembers; and if he has no cup of wine at the time of Kiddush he blesses shehecheyanu without a cup, and it appears to me that even if he blessed initially and did not bless on the second day of Yom Tov of the diaspora he blesses all seven — Magen Avraham. And Kaf HaChayyim wrote it appears the same applies to Shavuot, even though it is only two days, nevertheless it has make-up all seven. And further it appears to me that if he forgot to say shehecheyanu on the first night and on the second night said shehecheyanu, he need not say shehecheyanu again based on the first night to complete it — for if the first day is holy, then he completed shehecheyanu on the second day, and if the second day is holy, behold he said shehecheyanu — Kaf HaChayyim, see there. And if he forgot to say Kiddush at night, his law is as above siman 471 seif 8, and so too Eliyah Rabbah seif 485. And if he forgot to say the Haggadah he has no make-up at all, as it is written \"because of this\" at the hour when matzah and maror are placed before you, and so too Magen Avraham below siman 485 see there. And they do not say \"who performed miracles for us\" because he is destined to say it in the Haggadah — Tur and Kaf HaChayyim:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|1:ב": "(ב) <b>On Shabbat.</b> It is permitted to say the Haggadah from a book even though he is alone, and we are not concerned that perhaps he will lean, as written siman (479) [475] seif 9. Kaf HaChayyim:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|1:ג": "(ג) <b>He makes havdalah.</b> And if he remembered in the middle of the meal that he did not make havdalah, he makes havdalah in the middle of his meal over the cup; and if his intention was to drink wine during the meal he does not bless borei peri hagafen, and if it was not his intention he must bless, and he should not wait to make havdalah until after the meal, for then it would be like adding to the cups; and it is better that he say havdalah and Hallel on one cup, for one cannot have two sanctifications on one cup — only Kiddush and havdalah are permitted, for both are one matter; and if he forgot to make havdalah until after birkat hamazon he says Hallel and havdalah on one cup, for one cannot [do so] on another matter. Magen Avraham, and see above siman 296:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|1:ד": "(ד) <b>Kiddush.</b> See Acharonim on this. And the essence is as Rama ruled, so agreed Taz, that one who sees him washing hands thinks he will also bless al netilat yadayim as during the rest of the year; but in truth it is forbidden to bless, since this washing is not for eating matzah, for it is a great interruption, and so is custom:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|4:א": "(א) <b>Afterward.</b> It is good to have live and cooked [vegetables] over which one blesses borei peri ha'adamah, as written siman 203; and custom is to take what they call parsley. And it is good to take karpas, which is a notarikon: 60 (sixty) ten thousands worked hard labor, and they call it in foreign tongue epich; and so Acharonim, and see Magen Avraham:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|4:ב": "(ב) <b>Salt water.</b> And if [Pesach] falls on Shabbat he should make the salt water from erev Shabbat; and if he did not make it from erev Shabbat and has no vinegar he should make a little salt water, as written siman 321, Taz. And Magen Avraham wrote, and it appears to me that after he ate the vegetable it is permitted to remove the vegetable and salt water from the bowl:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|4:ג": "(ג) <b>For the chagigah.</b> Even if it falls on erev Pesach on Shabbat. And see in Rosh what he wrote in the name of Mahari of Orleans and Mahari of Corbeil that their words are the opposite of the Gemara, and see Yad Efraim on this:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|4:ד": "(ד) <b>And he arranges.</b> The order of the bowl of the Ari is copied from Etz Chayim, and thus: the order of the guarded [matzot] shall be in this order — one takes three guarded matzot and places Kohen on top, then Levi, and below him Yisrael, corresponding to the three mochin of Abba Ilaa; afterward one takes maror, karpas, charoset, and two kinds of cooked dish — a roasted lamb shank and a boiled egg — and places these five things above the three matzot: namely the shank, which was kindness at your right, and the egg, which is gevurah at your left; maror, which alludes to tiferet, in the middle between the shank and the egg, for tiferet mediates between kindness and gevurah; afterward charoset on the right line under the shank because it alluded to netzach; afterward karpas, which alludes to hod, place under the egg on your left line; afterward take chazeret and place below the maror on the middle line corresponding to yesod, in order to make afterward a korech with chazeret. And the bowl itself that includes them all is malchut — behold the ten sefirot of chochmah; and one should not change this order, and fortunate is one who intends for the above — end quote:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|4:ה": "(ה) <b>Shank.</b> And there should be a little meat on it. Darkei Moshe:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|4:ו": "(ו) <b>Roasted.</b> They eat it because it was a remembrance of mourning, but roasted meat they do not eat, and such is custom; and see Taz. And if he forgot to roast the meat or the egg on erev Yom Tov he should not roast at night unless his intention is to eat that night and day. Maharil and see Magen Avraham:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|5:א": "(א) <b>Chazeret.</b> It is lettuce; olshin is coriander; tamcha is horseradish. Custom spread to take horseradish for maror and bless on the leaves; and the stalk one takes for korech. In responsa Chacham Tzvi siman 119 he wrote: the chazeret mentioned in our Mishnah is the vegetable called in foreign tongue salad and in Spanish salad, and it has the signs mentioned in the Gemara; and because in Ashkenaz lands it is not found at Pesach time they did not become accustomed to take it for maror and take horseradish, which is tamcha, and one fulfills with it — though they cannot eat even half a kezayit because of its sharpness — and they nullify the mitzvah of maror — end quote, see there. And I also heard from one foreigner that in a hot country lettuce is found in Nissan days and is double and thick like what we call in these lands head lettuce, and they eat that species for maror; and see Kaf HaChayyim:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|5:ב": "(ב) <b>In the root.</b> Meaning small roots that split here and there, but the large root on which the vegetable stands is included in the stalk. Maharil, and see Taz:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|5:ג": "(ג) <b>For a kezayit.</b> For it is like half a chicken egg, and this measure is required in full apart from the space between the leaves, as written siman 486; and one should be careful about this, for otherwise it is a berachah lebatalah, since he blesses on eating it and eating implies a kezayit. One who is ill or fastidious may take for himself whichever kind of maror is most palatable to him; and if it is impossible for him altogether, in any case he should eat a little or chew in his mouth for remembrance of bitterness of taste without a berachah, and it is fit to push himself with all his ability even if it pains him to fulfill a mitzvah of the Sages, as written above siman 474 seif 10:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|5:ד": "(ד) <b>Bitter vegetable.</b> Even though one cannot [bless] on another matter, in any case one should bless only on these species enumerated in the Mishnah, and so agreed Shach and Magen Avraham. Kaf HaChayyim:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|5:ה": "(ה) <b>Vinegar.</b> And Pesach that falls on Shabbat — one must put the liquid in it from erev Shabbat; and if he forgot, one may do so through shinui, namely that he put the liquid first in the vessel and afterward put charoset in it, as written siman 321 seif 16, and so Magen Avraham; and see there in Taz sk 10 that he forbids putting ground baked matzah to mix with wine or honey water on Shabbat even through shinui, and his words are not compelling; also the world is not careful about this. Kaf HaChayyim:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|5:ו": "(ו) <b>For straw.</b> And one should not grind it well so that [the pieces] be long. Beit Yosef:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|6:א": "(א) <b>He washes.</b> For anything whose dipping is in liquid requires washing; and Taz wrote: from here is clear proof against those who are not careful all year to wash hands for things dipped in liquid — how is this night different from all nights of the year? And if he comes to purify himself more on this night, at least he should be careful also on days of repentance; but here there is only lack of care and they do not scrutinize to equalize their measures — end quote. And Eliyah Rabbah in siman 85 wrote the world is accustomed not to wash and they have on what to rely, that Tosafot hold so, and so Magen Avraham siman 158 see there; and that they wash on this night is like one of the other changes made this night so that they will ask. Kaf HaChayyim:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|6:ב": "(ב) <b>Less.</b> For if he eats a kezayit there will be doubt whether he blesses birkat hamazon — Tur; therefore if it happened that he ate a kezayit he should not bless birkat hamazon. Taz and Magen Avraham, Kaf HaChayyim:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|6:ג": "(ג) <b>For afikoman.</b> And the larger portion he hides. Acharonim. Also it is fit to wrap in a cloth and place between the pillow and mattress, and when he wishes to eat he takes it out as it was in the napkin and drapes it behind him and walks about four amot and says: thus were our ancestors going with their leftovers tied in their garments — Rashal. And see Kaf HaChayyim siman 474 sk 2 — reason for custom that children take the afikoman:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|6:ד": "(ד) <b>The bowl.</b> With everything on it, and one need not remove the cooked dishes. Tur:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|6:ה": "(ה) <b>Ha lachma.</b> Some say ha kolachma — see Magen Avraham. And Maharshal wrote siman 88 to say it in a loud voice:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|6:ו": "(ו) <b>To remove them.</b> And now they are not accustomed to remove them because children have no recognition in this that they know these things were not placed on the table for the purpose of eating. Magen Avraham:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|6:ז": "(ז) <b>And they will ask.</b> And he tells them they are not permitted to eat until they tell of the Exodus from Egypt; therefore one should not place any matzah on the table until after the seder. Maharil:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|7:א": "(א) <b>Why is this night different.</b> One should not say \"we are obligated to dip,\" etc., but rather \"we dip,\" for it is not an obligation but only for recognition — Tur. When he says \"and it is this that stood for our fathers,\" etc., until \"the Holy One, blessed be He, saves us from them,\" he should hold the cup in his hand. And when he says \"and we will say before Him song,\" it must be v'ne'emar with segol vowels (under the alef), which is past tense, referring to the redemption from Egypt that has passed. Bach, Rashal, Shelah:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|7:ב": "(ב) <b>For maror.</b> But when he reaches \"the Pesach that our fathers,\" etc., he should not hold it in his hand, for it is like raising consecrated things outside [the Temple], but nevertheless he should look at it. Maharil siman 193:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|7:ג": "(ג) <b>With his finger.</b> Written in Hagahot Minhagim that one should sprinkle with the small finger, namely the pinky; and Magen Avraham wrote one should sprinkle with the ring finger, which is next to the small, for with the ring finger the Holy One, blessed be He, struck Egypt. And in Darkei Moshe he wrote one should take specifically with the index finger in remembrance of what is said \"it is the finger of the Almighty,\" and such is the essence. Kaf HaChayyim:",
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt|7:ד": "(ד) <b>In his hand.</b> In the text of the berachah \"who redeemed us\" Maharil wrote: when Pesach falls on Shabbat one should reverse and say first \"from the Pesachim\" and afterward \"from the sacrifices,\" for the chagigah does not override Shabbat, and on the second night the opposite — see there. And in Pesach Me'ubin siman 268 he wrote one should say everything in one text \"from the sacrifices and from the Pesachim,\" and Acharonim agreed, such as Maharil, and so Bach. In saying Hallel one should not sit reclining but with awe and fear — Shelah. Levush wrote they are not accustomed to bless on Hallel since they divide half before the meal and half after. A blind person is obligated in reading the Haggadah, and when he reaches \"this matzah\" and \"this maror\" he does not say \"this\" but says \"the matzah we eat,\" etc. — Kaf HaChayyim sk 39:",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1:א": "Mishnah Pesachim 114",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1:ב": "So it implies daf 103 regarding washing hands",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1:ג": "Sefer Abudraham in the name of his teacher",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|2:א": "Conclusion of the Gemara daf 108",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|2:ב": "Tur in siman 474 in the name of Rav Sherira, Rav Hai, Rosh, and R. Yitzchak",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|3:א": "Mishnah 117",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|3:ב": "Kol Bo in the name of R. Yehonatan",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|4:א": "Tur in siman 479 in the name of Tosafot and Rosh",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|4:ב": "There in the Gemara 114",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|4:ג": "(ג) Tur, there in the laws of Pesach.",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|5:א": "Mishnah there 39",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|5:ב": "Hagahot Maimoni in the name of Rabbenu Tam and Semag",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|5:ג": "There in the Gemara",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|5:ד": "There in the Mishnah",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|5:ה": "There in the Gemara",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|5:ו": "Hagahah of Maimoni",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|6:א": "I cited it siman 128 seif 7",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|6:ב": "There in the Gemara 111",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|6:ג": "Rosh there, R. Yitzchak, and Shulchan Pesach, and responsum of Maharil — in order to remove himself from the dispute of poskim on birkat hamazon",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|6:ד": "Rosh and Mordechai and Shulchan Pesach",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|6:ה": "Tur and Rokeach",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|6:ו": "Rosh in responsum and Shulchan Pesach",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|6:ז": "There in chapter 115, and so Tosafot and Rashbam there",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|7:א": "Mishnah and baraita there 116",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|7:ב": "Tur from that which \"bread of affliction,\" etc., 115",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|7:ג": "There 111",
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|7:ד": "Tur in the name of custom of Ashkenaz and Shulchan Pesach",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1:א": "<b>Seif 1 and blesses.</b> As written in Eruvin 40b and Avodah Zarah:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1:ב": "<b>And if it falls on Shabbat.</b> See Tosafot Pesachim 106a s.v. zokhreihu, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1:ג": "<b>And if it falls.</b> There 103a:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1:ד": "<b>And if he forgot.</b> For havdalah requires a cup, as written there and in chapter 8 of Berachot; and the one who blesses must taste, as written in chapter erev Pesach and chapter 8 of Berachot; and since he began the Haggadah it is forbidden to drink, as written between the cups, etc., and as Ramban wrote in Milchamot and Ran, unlike Razah there; and Tosafot 103b s.v. Rav Ashi:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1:ה": "<b>And one does not, etc., and if.</b> See Tosafot Chagigah 18b s.v. hanotel, etc., and he wrote \"and one should not bless,\" etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1:ו": "<b>And for a common person.</b> And so one infers the language in the Mishnah: \"they poured for him the first cup,\" and so in all:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2:_": "<b>Seif 2 and he does not.</b> See siman 474 from what is written:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|3:_": "<b>Seif 3 and nevertheless.</b> Yerushalmi there: why — lest he become drunk? He is already drunk! What is the difference between wine within the meal and wine after the meal? Wine after the meal intoxicates; wine within the meal does not intoxicate. And since he did not challenge from before the meal, learn that before too it intoxicates, only it is not customary to drink much before eating:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:א": "<b>Seif 4 they bring before a common person.</b> There 115b, and the halachah is like R. Huna:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:ב": "<b>Bowl.</b> See Tosafot there s.v. lamah:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:ג": "<b>Three.</b> As written in Berachot 39b and Shabbat 116b: all agree on Pesach, etc., and in chapter erev Pesach: what is his way with a piece, etc.; and one needs another two whole [matzot] for lechem mishneh, as written there and there \"all agree on Shabbat,\" etc., and the same for Yom Tov — for on Yom Tov too it did not descend, as Rif wrote in chapter erev Pesach; and so we say at the beginning of Yom Tov 2b: \"and it was on the sixth day,\" etc. — weekday prepares for Shabbat, weekday prepares for Yom Tov, etc.; and see siman 529 seif 1, and this is the view of Rosh at the end of Pesachim and at the beginning of Pesachim and Mordechai; but the view of Rif chapter erev Pesach and Rambam chapter 8 Laws of Chametz and Matzah is that one needs only two matzot, and so Maggid Mishneh and many Geonim, and so Rambam; and so is the language of the Mishnah: \"they brought before him matzah,\" etc., and so \"bread of affliction\" implies so — for otherwise this is not a way of poverty since one can take two matzot and also half a matzah; rather as above; and he blesses hamotzi and al ha'michya on the piece and breaks both, and it is not like making bundles of mitzvot since it is berachot, as Tosafot wrote there s.v. hakol; and they are not called bundles, etc., see there; and so Rashba in responsum, and such is the essence:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:ד": "<b>Matzah and maror, etc.</b> Mishnah there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:ה": "<b>And karpas or, etc.</b> There: \"they brought before him\" — he dips, etc.; and Ran wrote when there are no other vegetables, and as written in the Gemara there; and see Magen Avraham:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:ו": "<b>And vinegar or.</b> Mishnah there: he dips in chazeret; and see Tosafot there s.v. matbil, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:ז": "<b>And two.</b> Mishnah there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:ח": "<b>One male.</b> Gemara there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:ט": "<b>Custom, etc., and the meat custom, etc.</b> Like Ravina there; and Ran wrote he disagrees with all; and see Ran; and there 116b \"this meat,\" etc.; and custom is egg in place of cooking; and Kol Bo wrote in the name of Yerushalmi they were accustomed to shank and egg; and see Beit Yosef:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:י": "<b>And he arranges, etc.</b> As written in chapter 3 of Yoma 32b; and see Tosafot there s.v. avurei and Mefareish, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:כ": "<b>Custom, etc., and the egg.</b> Per R. Yitzchak in Tosafot there s.v. shnei, and as stam Mishnah — these matters are not like ben Teima; and so Rambam:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:ל": "<b>On coals.</b> As written there 75a: \"he cut it,\" etc.; and in Beitza one cannot, for it requires a pomegranate skewer:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4:מ": "<b>And likewise roasted.</b> Like ben Teima, for stam Mishnah of erev Pesach is like him, as written in chapter 6 there; and as Tosafot wrote there and there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5:א": "<b>Seif 5 but not.</b> Since it taught only stalk:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5:ב": "<b>And if there is none.</b> As written there: \"but chazeret we learned\" — learn that he was particular about order:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5:ג": "<b>And if there is none.</b> Like Rav Huna there who ruled like the others:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5:ד": "<b>And charoset, etc., and afterward.</b> There 116a: therefore one needs, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5:ה": "<b>Red male.</b> Yerushalmi there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5:ו": "<b>And they make.</b> As written there: remembrance of tapuach; and Geonim explained see there that Israel are compared to this apple, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5:ז": "<b>And they place on it, etc.</b> There: spices, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:א": "<b>Seif 6 he washes hands.</b> There 115a-b:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:ב": "<b>And he should not bless.</b> See Tosafot there s.v. kol, etc.; and see siman 158 seif 4 that the essence is he should bless on netilat yadayim for something dipped in liquid — see there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:ג": "<b>And he takes.</b> Mishnah: he dips in chazeret, etc., as above:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:ד": "<b>Less than a kezayit.</b> Since he takes only for recognition, and they bless on less than a kezayit, as Tosafot wrote chapter 2 of Sukkah in the name of Yerushalmi, and as written in chapter 6 of Berachot; and one should not take a kezayit; and see Taz:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:ה": "<b>And he blesses.</b> Gemara there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:ו": "<b>And he does not bless afterward.</b> Meaning even if he ate a kezayit, as above; and it is from what is written 115a at first, etc., without a berachah — thus the first berachah exempts it; and see Rashbam 114b s.v. peshita, etc.; and see Tosafot 115a s.v. vehadar, etc., who disagree with Rashbam, and R. Yitzchak Tov Elem; but Rosh there explains like Rashbam; and Tosafot's question is from what is written 115b in the aggadah that Hallel was an interruption specifically for netilat yadayim but not for berachah, for even companions who removed [the table] it was not an interruption when they left some companions; and see Tosafot 102a s.v. ve'akru, etc.; and it is written in Seder HaPesach that the difference of these explanations is for birkat hamazon; and see Tosafot 100b s.v. yedei yayin, etc.; and it appears they follow their view and the dispute in siman 474; and see Rosh there sk 24; and it appears to me, etc., that it is different in birkat hamazon, etc., but if he stood, etc.; and see Tosafot 103b s.v. Rav; and if you ask, etc., and per my explanation, etc.; and see Ran; and so Taz; and therefore Shulchan Aruch ruled well according to his view, but according to Rabbenu Yonah there too one must bless:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:ז": "<b>And he takes matzah.</b> As above: what is the relevance, etc.; and he said the middle one, as written in Berachot and Shabbat there — a piece within the whole; and see what I wrote with Heaven's help:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:ח": "<b>And he places half.</b> That both eatings that he eats for the sake of matzah should be from the piece:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:ט": "<b>And he raises.</b> As written: matzah requires raising; and see Hagahot Maimonides end of Laws of Monetary Damages see there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:י": "<b>And he says in the language, etc.</b> As written: \"and you shall tell,\" etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6:כ": "<b>And then he commands.</b> There: why do they remove, etc.; and see Tosafot there s.v. lamah, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:א": "<b>Seif 7 immediately.</b> Meaning after removing the bowl immediately, so that they will ask; and as written above; and here the son asks, etc.; and as written:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:ב": "<b>If there is none.</b> Gemara there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:ג": "<b>And when the son.</b> There 115-116: he dismisses them, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:ד": "<b>And when he begins, etc.</b> See Tosafot 114a s.v. hevi'u, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:ה": "<b>And when he reaches \"therefore.\"</b> Midrash Shir HaShirim; and he said there in order to say song over the wine, as written in Arachin 11a; and Hallel is the song, as written there 10b and chapter 4 of Rosh Hashanah and chapter 1 of Megillah:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:ו": "<b>And custom is to sprinkle.</b> As written in Yerushalmi beginning chapter erev Pesach: from where four cups, etc.; and the Sages said corresponding to the four cups of punishment that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to give, etc. — see there:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:ז": "<b>With his finger.</b> See there: \"it is the finger of the Almighty:\"",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:ח": "<b>All sixteen times.</b> Magen Avraham:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:ט": "<b>And the bread shall be, etc.</b> As written there they answer him words:",
  "beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7:י": "<b>And then, etc.</b> As written above 102: \"and if he brought,\" etc.; and Rosh wrote there in the name of Yerushalmi: because of his shame that he is earlier in the verse and earlier for berachah:",
  "biur-halacha/part-001.txt|1:_": "Until he began — see in Mishna Berurah what we wrote that for us even before they began the law is likewise, and this is from the words of the Gra; and the Gra exaggerated further that even if he remembered before eating karpas he may eat karpas without havdalah for this reason. And this detail is seemingly not so clear, for the law of adding to the cups through a berachah is not mentioned in all poskim, only in the words of Ravyah and the implication of Tur in siman 472 that for Rosh there is no rule for this between the first two cups, even when he blesses; and if so, perhaps it is better not to be concerned for this concern and not to taste before havdalah, which is a clear prohibition according to all, especially since perhaps even Avi Ezri agrees that havdalah proves on this cup that it was not brought to add on the cups; and the law requires further study:",
  "biur-halacha/part-001.txt|3:_": "Permission is in his hand — and all this is between the cups; but if he poured the cup and began to expound on it in the Haggadah he is not permitted to interrupt in the middle — so Ramban in his hasagot, and so Ran; and it appears from his words even more that even if he has not yet begun the Haggadah, only that he poured the cup and prepared himself to say the Haggadah, it is also forbidden; and Baal HaMaor permits in all cases, and so Tosafot daf 103 that only in Hallel or in the berachah \"who redeemed us\" is it forbidden; and Mechaber was silent in seif 1 like the view of Ramban, even regarding a cup of mitzvah, and all the more a cup of permission; see in Beur HaGra there sk 4:",
  "biur-halacha/part-001.txt|5:_": "He takes wormwood — see in Mishna Berurah that we explained in the words of the gloss that wormwood is not among the five species of maror, and so is proven in the long Darkei Moshe see there in his explanation of the words of the Agur. However Magen Avraham and Shach disagreed with him and proved that the Agur's intent is that wormwood is one of the five species of maror and is the maror mentioned last among the five species; and in my humble opinion it requires further study, for as it appears wormwood is not fit for eating, as implied from several verses that describe every evil thing with wormwood, as written \"a root bearing gall and wormwood\" or \"its end bitter as wormwood,\" and we require something fit for eating by a person in any case, as explained in the words of Riaz and Ritva and as also proven from the Gemara that we require something purchasable with ma'aser money, unless impossible — then it is fit in any case somewhat for eating. Afterward I found in Rashbatz in Sefer Yavin Shemuah that he wrote explicitly one does not fulfill with wormwood because it is a type of tree and we require from types of vegetables, as we learned explicitly \"these vegetables,\" etc., and so explained in the Gemara that it challenges \"perhaps bitter caper,\" etc., and this is not like the view of Magen Avraham and not like the view of the gloss, for even if we say it has sap and one's face is ashamed, as we explained in Mishna Berurah, nevertheless these signs help only for a vegetable. Unless Rama did not write to fulfill with it as law but only as a general remembrance when one lacks the five species, and as the Gra also wrote; and the law requires further study. And know further that the sign that it has sap and one's face is ashamed — even though Rif and Rambam and Rosh omitted it, nevertheless it is mentioned in several poskim; see Riaz and Rashbatz and Maharam Chalavah who brought this sign for law; and in Beit Meir he wrote perhaps even Rif and those with him omitted it only because we are not expert in these signs well; therefore though it is difficult to bless based on these signs, nevertheless if one lacks the five species he should certainly seek any bitter species of vegetables that has the sign of sap and face ashamed, provided it is fit for eating by a person. And behold per what I wrote above on maror, if we require it be fit for eating, I now found in Peri Megadim he is somewhat doubtful whether one fulfills with maror even if not fit for eating, and he did not see the words of the aforementioned Rishonim; also the simple sense of the Gemara that we require it be purchasable with ma'aser money, as above (and see Rashi there); and what was difficult for him on the Gemara's challenge \"perhaps bitter caper\" — one may say this is the second answer of the Gemara even according to the view that we do not require purchasability with ma'aser money; but nevertheless it is not at all produce of the ground; but for the conclusion in truth we do not need the Gemara's change of \"produce of the ground,\" and such is the way of sugyot in many places:",
  "biur-halacha/part-001.txt|6:א": "Less than a kezayit — from here it implies that something dipped in liquid requires washing even for less than a kezayit, even for plain bread; Mechaber wrote in siman 158 seif 3 in the name of some that washing is not required for less than a kezayit, and this is not as we wrote there in Mishna Berurah sk 20; and I already noted this in the second printing in the margins; but now I found a Tanna who supports my words — namely Rashbatz in Sefer Yavin Shemuah 4:35, who wrote regarding karpas that according to Rambam we require a kezayit, and he wrote it is reasonable that since washing is required, certainly a kezayit is required, like eating plain bread — it is reasonable he is not obligated in washing, for certainly they did not decree in such a case because of concern for terumah; and likewise for fruits in something dipped in liquid; and Mateh Yehudah brought it exactly as our words; and the law requires further study:",
  "biur-halacha/part-001.txt|6:ב": "And he does not bless afterward — see in Mishna Berurah; and the Gra wrote in his beur that all this is according to Mechaber's view in siman 474 that he blesses only one berachah on the first two cups, and because Hallel and the Haggadah are not an interruption, therefore he does not bless birkat hamazon — for he relies on the berachah of karpas to eat maror without a berachah; but according to the gloss and our custom that he blesses each cup and cup, one cannot rely on the berachah of the first cup because he interrupted with Hallel and the Haggadah; and if so one cannot say we rely in eating maror on the berachah of karpas, since he interrupted with the Haggadah and Hallel (only maror is exempt from a berachah because it is like things that come within the meal, which are dismissed by the berachah of hamotzi); and if so he is obligated to bless birkat hamazon after eating karpas, since it is not related to the meal that comes afterward — this is the essence of the Gra's words. And in truth Rav Magen Avraham was careful about this and wrote in siman 474 that each cup requires a berachah according to all, even for one who holds the Haggadah and Hallel are not an interruption, and because each mitzvah is separate therefore he must bless on each (and as Rif wrote at the beginning of his words in the name of the rabbis); and per Magen Avraham's words the Gra's words are rejected, and as Magen Avraham wrote, many Acharonim wrote above in siman 278 seif 6 except Taz — see there; and one may say this reasoning applies here too, that karpas and maror are two mitzvot and therefore one does not exempt the other; and the law requires further study:",
  "chatam-sofer/part-001.txt|1:_": "(There in Magen Avraham sk 4) Maharil wrote they are accustomed to take karpas — n.b. in Greek and Latin: apia. And notarikon: El — O Worker of salvations are You; and in foreign tongue seller; and see Chiddushei Sofer Orach Chayim siman 132:",
  "chatam-sofer/part-001.txt|2:_": "In Mechaber seif 5 tamcha — n.b. notarikon: always tell the glory of the Almighty; and see responsa Orach Chayim siman 132 [there it is written in this language about the mitzvot of matzah and maror on Pesach — truly correct is what Chacham Tzvi wrote (and it is in siman 119), and the content of his words is that the chazeret mentioned in our Mishnah and in the language of the Sages is chasa, which is the head, and the first head mentioned in the Mishnah; and the mitzvah to return to it is the vegetable called in our country (salad), and so all my rabbis of blessed memory practiced and we practice after them; but I am accustomed to preach on Shabbat HaGadol: one who lacks designated people of fear who inspect and clean it of swarms of tiny worms found very much in Pesach days and not visible to weak eyes — therefore one who lacks in his house someone to inspect it properly should dip and take tamcha called horseradish; and if it is the third in the Mishnah and chasa is the first, nevertheless it is preferable from anywhere — Heaven forbid to stumble in a prohibition or many prohibitions even in safeik because of fulfilling a positive rabbinic mitzvah of maror in our time, which is rabbinic, especially since both can be fulfilled through tamcha] and we fulfill with stalks even dry and also with leaves — the view of Peri Chadash is that one fulfills with dried, and it is known by sense that dried and withered do not retain their taste in it and its smell is bitter much more than pickled in water from time to time, which Magen Avraham forbids below sk 14 and Kaf HaChayyim disagrees that water does not nullify taste, only pickling in vinegar; nevertheless it is also clear that honey is worse than pickling in vinegar so that it stands on the fire and begins to boil; and therefore it must be that there it is different, for bitter taste emerges through another power mixed in, namely the power of vinegar or even only water, unlike as long as the vegetable is by itself without another power it does not lose as long as some of its own bitterness is tasted — unlike dried leaves for those who forbid; and this is the reason they are accustomed to salt tamcha to nullify its bitterness somewhat so they can eat a kezayit of it; and so when we reclined at the table our master the pious one of priestly lineage, the honor of our teacher R. Nathan Adler, in the year 5545 in Vienna, no chasa was found for dipping without worms, and he commanded to salt tamcha and we ate it; and I expanded on this unlike the words of the Gaon in the Haggadah miracle story at first that the Gemara Berachot 39a forbids prima prima zita to sweeten taste — it is a fortiori from boiling in water see there; and per my view his a fortiori does not apply. And further it appears to me that if possible to eat Pesach and matzah with wormwood or even tamcha, which is not the normal way of eating, as Rambam wrote that mixing a bitter thing is not the normal way of eating — rather they nullify taste through prima zuta; and see below siman 475 in Magen Avraham sk 10; and in truth crushed tamcha dipped in vinegar is eaten thus, and it must be if one soaks it in vinegar again he should not bless on eating maror anyway after he filled his belly he should not bless again on eating maror:",
  "chatam-sofer/part-001.txt|3:_": "(There in Magen Avraham sk 14 in the matter of) Maharil wrote, etc., and all the more when rooted — n.b. and per Tosafot Rosh Hashanah 13a that a non-Jew has rights in what he planted — one may say a vegetable that does not last and is sown all year a non-Jew has rights, unlike a tree that lasts, for perhaps it still stands from its original owners and is not stolen — it is simple. However one should examine Bava Metzia 101b because of denial of land see there; and examine carefully:",
  "chatam-sofer/part-001.txt|4:_": "(There) in Mechaber seif 7 so that they will ask — n.b. the language of Rambam chapter 8 law 2: \"and here the son asks and says; the reader: why is this night different,\" etc. — it implies the reader of the Haggadah says \"why is this night different.\" And in the Gemara it implies if the son asks the reader should not say but rather \"we were slaves:\"",
  "chatam-sofer/part-001.txt|5:_": "(There) in Magen Avraham sk 29 called ah\"ch — n.b. yud-vav hei-kaf, meaning etzba (finger); gematria 163 in milui Y-H-V-H; and on the finger ten plagues reach — each plague sixteen, and three hundreds which are the weapons of sixteen; and the tablets with the finger of the Almighty reach — each utterance sixteen, as the Sages said: the sword and the book descended rolled from heaven:",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|1:א": "<b>And he blesses Shehecheyanu.</b> And if he did not say it on day one he blesses on day two or on one of the other days when he remembers; and if he has no cup of wine at the time of Kiddush he blesses shehecheyanu without a cup — Riaz in Shaarei Geulah chapter 2 of every erev; and so Magen Avraham, and it is simple in the Gemara chapter Lulav and Aravah, and similarly explained in Laws of Chanukah siman 677 see there; and Magen Avraham further wrote, and it appears to me the same if he forgot to say shehecheyanu on the second night he blesses all seven — end quote; and it appears the same for Shavuot, even though only two days, nevertheless it has make-up all seven. And further it appears simple that if he forgot to say shehecheyanu on the first night and on the second night said shehecheyanu, he need not say shehecheyanu again based on the first night to complete — for if the first day is holy he completed shehecheyanu on the second day, and if the second day is holy behold he said shehecheyanu; and if he forgot to say Kiddush at night his law is as above siman 471 seif 8, and so Eliyah Rabbah below seif 485; and if he forgot to say the Haggadah it is obvious he has no make-up at all, as written \"because of this\" when matzah and maror are before you, and so Magen Avraham below siman 485; and he challenged Eliyah Rabbah on this, that he understood in his words it refers to the Haggadah, and this is not so — he only wrote the law if he forgot Kiddush, as written:",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|1:ב": "<b>Shehecheyanu</b> and they do not say \"who performed miracles for us\" because he will say it in the Haggadah — so Tur and Maharil; Maharil wrote another reason see there; and Rokeach and Agudah wrote — all is in it: that they do not say the berachah \"who commanded us to drink four cups\" because each is a mitzvah by itself and there is interruption:",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|1:ג": "<b>And if it falls on Shabbat.</b> It is permitted to say the Haggadah on Pesach even alone, and we are not concerned he will lean, as explained above siman 471 seif 9, and not like the view of Maharil siman 193 who forbids:",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|1:ד": "<b>He says Yekum Purkan.</b> And they do not bless on spices every motzei Shabbat for Yom Tov, and many reasons were said — see in Tosafot and Rosh chapter erev Pesach; and Magen Avraham wrote they say havdalah seated like Kiddush, and so is custom to recline; and it appears to me the reason as explained above siman 322 that Hallel they say only standing; nevertheless on Pesach night they say it reclining, in the way of reclining and freedom — if so, the same for Kiddush and havdalah; and not like one who wrote one should stand when it falls on Shabbat, as written:",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|1:ה": "<b>He completes the Haggadah.</b> Levush wrote: \"and he should not interrupt the Haggadah\" — it implies the reason is it is forbidden to interrupt during the Haggadah; but this I did not find in any posek, and also why should he not interrupt in telling the Haggadah for the mitzvah of havdalah (and see above siman 472 sk 2), especially between sections; rather it appears to me the reason is simple: havdalah requires a cup; if he makes havdalah during saying the Haggadah he will interrupt afterward between havdalah and the berachah on the cup with saying the Haggadah; and it is better he bless on the cup and pour another cup for saying the Haggadah, because this appears like adding to the cup since he took a fixed cup for havdalah; and since it is possible in another way we act accordingly; and see in seif 3:",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|1:ו": "<b>And afterward he makes havdalah.</b> And Magen Avraham wrote: and if he remembered in the middle of his meal that he had not made havdalah, he makes havdalah immediately on the cup and recites borei peri hagafen if he did not have in mind to drink wine during the meal; and he should not wait to make havdalah until after the meal, for that is like adding to the cups, for one may not say havdalah and Hallel on one cup. And if he forgot until after Birkat HaMazon, he says Hallel and havdalah on one cup, since it is impossible otherwise — end of his words, Magen Avraham; and see there siman 496.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|1:ז": "<b>And one should not wash the hands at all before kiddush.</b> Behold, the later authorities multiplied opinions on this; and in my humble opinion the essential ruling follows the Mechaber's decision, and so agreed the Taz — that one who sees him washing his hands will think he also recites al netilat yadayim as during the rest of the year; but in truth it is forbidden to recite a berakha, since this washing is not for eating matzah, for it is a great interruption.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|1:ח": "<b>He should not pour for himself.</b> This too is the Mechaber's intent in what he wrote in his wording: and they pour for him through others.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|2:_": "<b>He drinks reclining.</b> And if he drank without reclining, see there siman 472 seif 7 and what is written there.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|3:א": "<b>Permission is in his hand.</b> According to what the Mechaber wrote below siman 474 in the gloss — that our custom is to recite a first berakha on each and every cup, which is not according to the Rash's view — if so, that which is permitted to drink between the cups is specifically when he had it in mind in a manner that he need not recite borei peri hagafen on it; otherwise it is like adding to the cups, as the Tur wrote in the name of R' Meir of Rothenburg, and so is the Bach's conclusion even according to the Rash's view — see there.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|3:ב": "<b>So that he not become intoxicated.</b> And according to this it implies that other beverages that are not intoxicating are permitted to drink; however, according to what the Mordekhai wrote — the reason that between the first and second it is permitted to drink is that wine whets the appetite and he will eat matzah with appetite — it is possible that other beverages are forbidden to drink between the first and second, since they do not whet; however, we have not found any posek who had a concern of prohibition in this, only regarding wine it was necessary to permit it so it not be like adding to the cups, and also so you not say \"wine satisfies, satisfies\"; and see there siman 471.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|4:א": "<b>And karpas.</b> Or another vegetable — meaning that it is eaten raw and cooked, on which one recites borei peri ha-adamah, as above siman 200; and the custom is to take what they call parsley; and it is good to take karpas, which is a notarikon: sixty ten thousands did arduous labor, and they call it in foreign tongue eppich; and so wrote the later authorities.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|4:ב": "<b>Or salt water.</b> And if Pesach falls on Shabbat, he should make the salt water on erev Shabbat; and if he did not make it on erev Shabbat and he has no vinegar, he should make a little salt water, as explained above siman 321.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|4:ג": "<b>And one in remembrance of the festival offering.</b> Even if Pesach falls on Shabbat, the Tur also concludes that he takes two cooked dishes, not according to the Rash's view; and it requires study, for in the Rash's abridged rulings he copied that one takes two cooked dishes even on the first day of Yom Tov, while the Rash's view there is actually the opposite.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|4:ד": "<b>In remembrance of the festival offering:</b> And the Rokeach wrote: there are those who say that one takes two cooked dishes in remembrance of Moshe and Aharon.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|4:ה": "<b>And charoset.</b> And they should be closer. In the Shulchan Aruch it is printed here in the name of Maharil, but in truth in Maharil I did not find this; rather he wrote, and these are his words: the eppich vegetable is fit to place so it be closer to it than the matzot, for if the matzot were close he would not need to break the afikoman from the first one so as not to pass over the matzot — end of his words. But charoset, meat, and egg were not mentioned at all, since one does not recite a berakha on them but only brings them on the table as a mere remembrance — the rule of not passing over the matzot does not apply to them at all. And there is one who says charoset is not a mitzvah, as in the mishnah in the chapter Arvei Pesachim; and Maharil was particular only regarding matzah, which is a mitzvah on which one recites a berakha and which we were commanded, and it is also prior in the order of berakhot — therefore one should be particular to bring the vegetables closer to him than the dishes; and even maror was not mentioned at all in Maharil to distance or bring close, even though one recites a berakha on it and was commanded; and it is possible that since it is not beloved to a person and has no precedence law in berakhot, Maharil was not particular about maror. Also the proof from the western lamp that Maharil brought there — one may distinguish; see there.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|4:ו": "<b>And likewise roasted.</b> And they eat it because it is a remembrance of mourning, as explained below siman 476; and the roasted meat one does not eat — and such is the custom — even in a place where they were accustomed to eat other roasted foods, because it is a remembrance of the Pesach offering; (and so is the Taz's conclusion, that this is the essential ruling). And if he forgot to roast the meat or the egg on erev Yom Tov, he should not roast at night unless he had in mind to eat it that night and day (Mahari\"v siman 193).",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:א": "<b>Lettuce (chazeret).</b> Since not everyone is expert in them and their names, and the language also changes according to countries, and not all vegetables are found in every place — therefore I said to copy here all the explanations of these vegetables as I found among the poskim, so that each person according to his language and country may understand the matter clearly. Chazeret is explained in the Gemara in Pesachim that it is chasa (lettuce); Rashi: latugi; the Arukh: latok; in Hagahot Maimoniot: lettuce or latukh; the Levush wrote that it is what we call in these countries zarzich; and the Mechaber's brother in Sha'ar HaTziyon wrote in the name of \"I found written\" that he challenged the Levush on this — only that it is called in foreign tongue latukh, and in Romance lektuka; and in medical books seven types are found from it — and there is no connection at all to zarzich found in these countries — end of his words. And in Berakhot chapter HaRo'eh regarding Bar Hedya interpreting dreams: \"tired are your affairs like lettuce\" — Rashi: that it is doubled and thick; and I heard from a foreigner that in a hot foreign land lettuce is found in Nissan days and is doubled and thick like what we call in these countries latif in foreign tongue, and they eat it — this is the species for maror. And see in Beit Yosef that he wrote: the mitzvah is with chazeret even though it is sweet and has no bitterness — therefore; and see there in chapter HaRo'eh regarding Bar Hedya, who said to Rava: your affair is bitter — it must be that nevertheless it has some bitterness. And in the Arukh: chazeret galin is bitter lettuce. And in the Agudah: if latugi is not found, take maraytikh — end of his words. Olashin in the Gemara: hindbi; Rashi: krishfla; the Arukh: olashin that satisfy; in foreign tongue sintzoni; and some say krispinyo — end of his words. And in Shulchan Shel Maharil I found that it was estrula; and some say in foreign tongue amatika andibi; and so Ra'avad — it is andibi, and it is found among us that they cook it and eat it with sugar and vinegar to nullify the bitterness. In Hagahot Maimoniot it is korvil, also found among us; but in Maharil, since in the siddurim it says to take korvil for the first dipping, it implies one does not fulfill maror with it. Tamcha — Rashi: marubya; Arukh: kardoya mariyo; later authorities wrote it is what they call in foreign tongue krien or maertikh; and so Tosefet Yom Tov; and see there what he challenged on Ra'avad; and so the custom spread in these countries to take krien for maror and to recite the berakha on the leaves, and for korekh they take the stalk. Charchevina is in the Gemara: atzvata dedikla; Rashi: videlah; Rokeach: agroryin. Maror in the Gemara: mararita; Rashi: amirfoyil in Poritz; Agudah: vermut, meaning wormwood.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:ב": "<b>Unless they are moist.</b> It implies that even if they are not completely dry, only wilted, one does not fulfill with them since they are not so moist — so Ri'az in Shibolei HaLeket; and so is the implication of most poskim, and so in Kol Bo. And that which the Gemara says one fulfills with wilted — it refers to stalks, as the Gemara concludes; and with stalks, since we hold one fulfills with dry, all the more so with wilted one fulfills according to his view; and although this is very obvious, I wrote it because Magen Avraham wrote on that which stalks fulfill whether moist — it implies one does not fulfill with wilted, and so implies Tur and Rambam — end of his words; and it is a printing error, an error that is evident, and it refers to leaves, as written; and see there siman 336 whether if one buries them in earth from before Yom Tov so they not wilt, it is permitted to take them — see there.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:ג": "<b>But not pickled.</b> Rashi explains: in vinegar; and the later authorities wrote that Rashi follows his view, as Beit Yosef wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 in his name — that pickling in water alone is not pickling, only in vinegar and brine; but we hold that something soaked in water from the eve is considered pickled like cooked — therefore they wrote one should protest those who soak maror and leave it so from the eve, for one does not fulfill with it. And in my humble opinion it appears that the custom of Israel is Torah (that they soak it so the leaves be moist and not wilted, as in s\"k 19), and they have support — for even to the poskim regarding prohibition of being pickled in water from the eve, for absorption and expulsion — nevertheless here they agree that specifically in vinegar it becomes pickled, as the Gemara says there daf 39: this marorita — the general rule: anything that has bitter taste one fulfills with; anything not pickled in vinegar still has bitter taste; and so the matter appears to the eye and senses. Proof: the mishnah and braita there teach: but not pickled, not scalded, not cooked; Tosafot there: not scalded and not cooked implies scalding is more than cooking — if not so it would be \"this and all the more so this,\" and so in many places — end of Tosafot; and so agreed the poskim. Still a difficulty: at least it should be \"this and all the more so this\" at the beginning — \"but not pickled,\" which is less than cooking, and all the more so scalding and cooking; at the end \"but not scalded and not cooked\" should be \"not this nor this,\" since scalding is more than cooking. Rather certainly this pickling refers to pickling in vinegar, per Rashi; and vinegar changes and nullifies bitterness more than scalding and cooking in water. If so, the whole mishnah is brought in one category by way of \"not this nor this\"; and now the general rule: to include pickling in water, since it retains bitter taste one fulfills — except per every rule I wrote in my book Ma'aseh Yesharim — many poskim hold no pickling law in water from the eve; even though for prohibition we are stringent, nevertheless for maror nowadays, which is rabbinic, it is also a stringency that leads to leniency that they eat when wilted; also we do not find in any early posek who wrote for prohibition in pickling in water — there is clear proof to permit; therefore one should not be stringent as above; and so one may say below siman 521 — see there.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:ד": "<b>Combine to a kezayit.</b> Which is like half an egg of a hen; and this measure is required in completeness, apart from the space between the leaves, as stated below siman 486; and one should be careful in this, for otherwise it is a berakha in vain, since he recites a berakha on eating and eating implies a kezayit. One who is ill or a nauseous person may take for himself whichever maror species is less bitter for him; and if he cannot at all, at least he eats a little or chews in his mouth to remember the taste of bitterness without a berakha; and it is fit to exert himself with all his ability even if it pains him, to fulfill the mitzvah of the Sages, as written siman 472 s\"k 22 — see there.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:ה": "<b>And the essential mitzvah is with chazeret,</b> and Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Semak: even to buy at expensive prices — meaning up to a third of the mitzvah; see in Magen Avraham what he wrote in the name of Mahari\"v; and whether a Jew may cut the maror himself — see above siman 494 s\"k 7.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:ו": "<b>Wormwood.</b> See there s\"k 18 that I wrote in the name of the Agudah that this is the maror mentioned in the Gemara.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:ז": "<b>Or another bitter vegetable.</b> Nevertheless, even though it is impossible otherwise, at least one should not recite a berakha except on these species enumerated in the mishnah, since it teaches \"these vegetables\"; and so is the implication of all poskim — specifically these; and so agreed Shach and Magen Avraham.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:ח": "<b>And charoset.</b> He should make it thick; Rokeach siman 266: and they make charoset on the sixth day, or if he wishes on erev Yom Tov which is Yom Tov, permission is in his hand, as in chapter Arvei Pesachim daf 117 — charoset is a mitzvah and not a preparatory mitzvah — end of his words. That which he ruled charoset is a mitzvah is in remembrance of clay and blood, as the Mechaber wrote, and per the Rash in chapter Arvei Pesachim, and so Rambam in his book, and Tur siman 475; nevertheless one does not recite a berakha on it, since it is subordinate to maror, as Tur wrote in siman 472 — unlike the implication of Rambam in his mishnah commentary; see Bach siman 471. And great wonder on the Levush, who in siman 471 seif 2 ruled plainly that charoset is not a mitzvah at all, while in this siman and in siman 475 ruled the opposite, as those who say it is a mitzvah — it requires study.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:ט": "<b>A little vinegar.</b> Agudah's wording: and on the table one grinds it; and if Pesach occurs on Shabbat, he must put the liquid in it from erev Shabbat; and if he forgot, he may do it through a change — meaning he puts the liquid first in the vessel and afterward puts the charoset in it, as explained above siman 328 seif 16; and so Magen Avraham; and see Taz s\"k 10, who forbids putting baked matzah meal to mix with wine or honey water on Shabbat even through a change — his words are not compelling; also the world is not careful about this.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|5:י": "<b>Similar to straw.</b> And one should not grind them well so they be long. Beit Yosef.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|6:א": "<b>He washes the hands.</b> Taz wrote: from here there is open reproof to those not careful all year to wash hands for things dipped in liquid; and if one comes to purify himself more on this night, at least he should be careful also on days of repentance — only here there is lack of vigilance and they do not precisely align their measures — end of his words. HaLevush in chapter 25 wrote: the world is accustomed not to wash and they have support — Tosafot hold so — end of his words; and this is the view of Maharam of Rothenburg that the Tur brought here (and Shakh challenged Maharam in vain); and so Magen Avraham siman 188. And that they wash on this night is like one of the other changes made this night so they will ask.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|6:ב": "<b>And he does not recite a berakha after it.</b> Even though he ate a kezayit — Bach wrote: it appears one who wishes to fulfill the obligation according to all geonim should eat a full kezayit in this first dipping, and have in mind at borei peri ha-adamah not to exempt maror with this berakha; thereby he is obligated to recite after the first dipping al achilat sh'ar — according to his view; and afterward when he recites ha-motzi he should also intend not to exempt maror with ha-motzi, and return to recite borei peri ha-adamah on maror and al achilat sh'ar — then he fulfills all views, and there is no unnecessary berakha since he does so to remove himself from dispute — end of his words. Still he does not fulfill according to Semag siman 220, that one does not recite on maror since he does not come to enjoy but only as a remembrance; therefore it appears to me the essential ruling is as most early authorities and Maharil siman 59 and Magen Avraham — not to recite at all.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|6:ג": "<b>And he gives half.</b> Later authorities wrote: meaning the majority of the matzah.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|6:ד": "<b>And he lifts the plate</b> with everything on it; and some are accustomed to place the afikoman bundle in the cloth on their shoulders in remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt. Peri Megadim siman 245: it is proper to lift the plate in one hand and the matzah in the other and say Ha Lachma Anya, etc. — end of his words.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|6:ה": "<b>Ha Lachma Anya.</b> And some say Ha Kolachma — see Magen Avraham; Maharshal siman 88: to say it in a loud voice.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|6:ו": "<b>He commands to remove them.</b> Magen Avraham wrote: now they are not accustomed to remove them — therefore; and some are accustomed to say the plate should be removed even though they do not remove; Peri Megadim siman 249: men of action open the door when saying Kol Dikhfin — if the door is closed how will they come — end of his words; Ra'avan wrote it refers to household members, not others who would come.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|7:א": "<b>Immediately the second cup.</b> Maharshal in responsum siman 88: rinsing and washing are not required — and so is the custom; and he does not hold the cup until reaching Lefichach, as the Mechaber wrote shortly; Shlah wrote that also when saying the passage \"and it stood for our fathers and for us\" until \"and Hashem saves us from them\" he should hold the cup, and then the bread is covered when holding the cup.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|7:ב": "<b>Mah Nishtanah.</b> Maharil wrote: one says it in a fine melody to praise the Blessed Name; and if erev Pesach is Shabbat, they hurry in everything to eat the afikoman before midnight and that the children not sleep, for they must then remain through the night before beginning kiddush — end of his words; Shlah wrote one should not say the Hagadah reclining but in awe and fear — end of his words; and when saying \"and we shall say before Him shirah,\" say venomar with segol vowel, past tense — for future redemption one does not say shirah, which is feminine, as in the midrash. Agreement of later authorities.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|7:ג": "<b>And likewise when reaching maror.</b> Unlike when reaching the Pesach our fathers had — he should not hold it in his hand, for it is like raising consecrated items outside; but nevertheless he should look at it. Mahari\"v siman 193.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|7:ד": "<b>With his finger.</b> Hagahot Minhagim: one should sprinkle with the small finger, meaning the pinky; Magen Avraham: sprinkle with the fist next to the small finger, as in Yalkut; Darchei Moshe: take specifically with the finger in remembrance of \"it is the finger of Hashem\"; and so is essential. Pesach Ma'uvin siman 261: some pour out wine and rinse the cup after sprinkling with the finger and wash hands, lest they drink what remembered the plagues, and also the finger was dipped and is repulsive — end of his words; in these countries they are not accustomed thus, and one should not change unless very nauseous — there is loss of food; see siman 171; this custom was not mentioned in any early posek.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|7:ה": "<b>While saying the Hagadah.</b> Maharil: the matzot should be covered until beginning Avadim — then he reveals them a little from above, to fulfill lechem oni that they answer upon it; also one should not place any matzah on the table besides the three matzot until after finishing the seder, otherwise how is lifting the plate considered lifting the table — end of his words. \"Until after the seder\" is not precise, for when beginning Avadim he returns the plate to its place and then may place other matzot on it too.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|7:ו": "<b>Who says the Hagadah.</b> See Beit Yosef that a blind person is also obligated in reading the Hagadah, unlike Ri'az and responsum Radbaz siman 59; and so is the Gemara's conclusion in Pesachim daf 115 — obligated in Hagadah and can discharge others; see Sha'ar Efraim siman 27 who resolves Ri'az's words; nevertheless for practical law he agrees with Beit Yosef's conclusion; still it appears to me when reaching \"this matzah\" and \"this maror\" he should say \"this,\" as the Gemara says there — \"this\" comes to exclude the blind — it implies he should recognize it by sight, as Rashbam explained there; even though per the conclusion there one may distinguish — nevertheless one may say in short formula \"matzah that we eat,\" etc.; see Bach siman 464, who wrote in the name of Maharshal about one who was imprisoned and did not know Hagadah but had a humash and said all parashiyot of the Exodus — end of his words.",
  "chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|7:ז": "<b>The cup in his hand.</b> In the text of the berakha Asher Ga'alanu, Mahari\"v wrote: when Pesach falls on the first day of Yom Tov, reverse and say first from the Pesach offerings and then from the sacrifices; on the second night the opposite; Peri Megadim siman 261: say everything in one formula from sacrifices and Pesach offerings; agreement of later authorities like Mahari\"v.",
  "chokhmat-shlomo/part-001.txt|1:_": "<b>Seif 5 — These are the vegetables with which one fulfills his obligation, etc.</b> Note: we read in Pesachim daf 39b: Rambach asked whether one fulfills with ma'aser sheni for maror, etc., until Rava said it is reasonable — matzah and maror with Yom Tov — see my explanation of this sugya in my books on Orach Chayim from the year 5622 in the laws of Birkat HaMazon siman 184, in my writings there daf 131a — see there and investigate.<br><b>(There, seif 5 in the gloss)</b> And if he lacks one of these vegetables he takes wormwood or another bitter vegetable, etc. Note: it appears clear to me that one may take radish called rettich in our country for maror, for it is explicit in Magen Avraham and Beit Yosef that tamcha is what they call maraytikh; and if in Ashkenaz they call krien rettich, nevertheless rettich too is so called; if so, both are called thus — only to distinguish they call one maraytikh and one rettich, but their root is one; reason supports this, for radish too is pungent like krien, soft at first and hard at end, a sharp thing like krien; and Rama's view is that otherwise one fulfills with any bitter vegetable, and Rashi in Chumash as Magen Avraham wrote. This does not contradict the mishnah — one may say: for matzah types where the Torah specifies importance, only these five count as bread; but maror is not specified in Torah what it is, therefore Rashi explained from Torah any bitter herb is maror, but the Sages enumerated those common in their place, soft beginning hard end — they found these and enumerated them. Similarly Megillah 10: why did the Sages enumerate these, etc. — they found these and enumerated, not only these; so too maror — they found these and enumerated; and if another similar species is found it is also maror; therefore Rashi: since Torah did not specify the species, whatever is a bitter vegetable is called maror — unlike matzah where Torah requires chimutz and it be called bread, and Torah specified bread is only wheat and barley — therefore for matzah we need specifically these; not so maror; therefore for rettich, which we doubt if in tamcha category, there is room to say perhaps in tamcha; and if you say not in tamcha, perhaps per Rashi and Rama one fulfills with any thing. The essential view: both krien and rettich are in tamcha category and are one species as explained — several elders told me they saw people fulfill maror with rettich; investigate.",
  "eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt|1:_": "[1] <b>[Levush] And one need not wash, etc.</b> So wrote Rama, and Taz agreed, and so is widespread custom not to wash; and if hands are not clean he washes a little but does not recite on washing; for first dipping wash without berakha, for second dipping wash with berakha. What Magen Avraham challenged — that all year one does not wash hands for dipping — is no difficulty, as Levush wrote seif 6; and this is Maharil's wording: if one washes before kiddush it appears the law is like Beit Shammai who washes hands and then sanctifies — until here. So he explained what Darkei Moshe wrote — that Beit Shammai's view is kiddush requires washing, meaning they wash before pouring the cup, i.e., kiddush; investigate, for I abbreviated; Magen Avraham wondered in vain; Ma'agalei Tzedek wrote the reason: Hagadah recitation is an interruption between washing and eating.",
  "eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt|2:_": "[2] <b>And he makes kiddush, etc.</b> And if he forgot, his law is as above siman 471 seif 8; but if he forgot to say Hagadah he has no compensation, for it is written \"because of this\" when matzah and maror are placed before you (later authorities).",
  "eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt|3:_": "[3] <b>Sheheheyanu, etc.</b> Chok Yaakov wrote: if he did not bless on the first night and they said it on the second night, he fulfills either way — if the first was holy he completed on the second day; if the first was weekday he need not complete; if they said on the first and not the second, he blesses all seven when he remembers; see siman 494 s\"k.",
  "eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt|4:_": "[4] <b>[Levush] Because he will say, etc.</b> Maharil wrote one does not recite she-asah nissim except on a rabbinic mitzvah like Chanukah and Purim; Matah Moshe wrote in the name of Rav Amram that it is a day of salvation and preferable to miracles.",
  "eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt|5:_": "[5] <b>[Levush] Havdalah, etc.</b> Seated (Matah Moshe), like Hallel all year standing and on Pesach night sitting because of reclining and freedom as written end of siman 422; therefore one should not stand even at the beginning of Vaykhulu.",
  "eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt|6:_": "[6] <b>[Levush] The seven havdalot, etc.</b> In Avudraham he explained: between holy and profane, light and darkness, Israel and nations — behold three; \"between the seventh day and six days of work\" is not counted but since we say it generally we say it here; between sanctity of Shabbat and sanctity of Yom Tov you distinguished — this is ochel nefesh permitted on Yom Tov, behold four; \"and the seventh day from six days of work you sanctified\" — this is the last Yom Tov of the festival separated from the six intermediate days — behold five; \"and You distinguished Your people,\" etc. — are two distinctions between kohanim and levi'im and levi'im and Israelites; difficulty: where are the six intermediate days — strained to reconcile.",
  "eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt|7:_": "[7] <b>And afterward he makes havdalah, etc.</b> Then he makes havdalah on the cup; if he remembered in the middle of the meal he makes havdalah immediately on the cup and does not recite borei peri hagafen; if he forgot until after Birkat HaMazon he says Hallel and havdalah on one cup, since it is impossible otherwise.",
  "eshel-avraham/part-001.txt|4:_": "<b>And two cooked dishes.</b> Tur concludes even if Pesach falls on the first day of Yom Tov; see Ba'er Heitev chapter 1:6 and Likutei 8:71 siman 22, who challenged Tur's words against each other.",
  "eshel-avraham/part-001.txt|6:_": "<b>That the women understand.</b> In truth women are not obligated in reciting the Hagadah, which is a rabbinic mitzvah that women were also in that miracle — that reason does not apply to a Torah mitzvah; it would apply only to sukkah, though they were also covered by the cloud. Rather our obligation is to tell them Hashem's miracles and wonders; see Tur in Yoreh De'ah siman 253.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|1:_": "<b>(1) [Seif 1] They pour for him the first cup, etc.</b> And the cups require rinsing and washing, for they are cups of berakha — Drashot Maharil Seder Ha'agadah; Rokeach siman 283; and so Mahariv in the name of Maharai and Beit Yosef Darchei Moshe in siman 486 s\"k 2; and Chayei Adam kelal 130 s\"k 10 wrote: if they are clean, rinsing and washing between cups are not needed, only rinsing the first cup suffices — end of his words; nevertheless see above siman 183 seif 1 and our words there s\"k 4, that per Zohar and tikkunim a Birkat HaMazon cup requires washing and rinsing even if clean — see there; nevertheless it is good to have a vessel full of water near the table to rinse and wash cups between cup and cup. Sefer HaMitzvot; Tosafot Chayim on Chayei Adam there s\"k 34; and see further below s\"k 142 and siman 479 s\"k 1.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|2:_": "<b>(2) There. They pour the first cup, etc.</b> See above siman 472 s\"k 58 and below siman 474 s\"k 2.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|3:_": "<b>(3) There. And he makes kiddush on it, etc.</b> And before kiddush one should say this text: for the sake of the unification of Kudsha Berikh Hu (<b>vav heh yod heh vav heh</b>) in fear and love (<b>yod heh yod heh vav heh heh</b>) love and fear (<b>aleph heh heh yod vav heh heh</b>) to unify the name yod heh with vav heh in complete unity (<b>yod heh vav heh</b>) in the name of all Israel and in the name of every soul, spirit, and living nefesh, unique soul related to the roots of our nefesh, ruach, neshamah, chayah, yechidah, garments, those close and attached, from the totality of Atzilut, Beriah, Yetzirah, Asiyah and every detail of ABY\"A — each parzuf and sefirah of ABY\"A details — behold we come to fulfill with intent all mitzvot operative this night, the Pesach night, first night of the Festival of Matzot: mitzvah of kiddush on the first wine cup; mitzvah of first hand-washing for karpas dipping; eating karpas; rabbinic mitzvah of telling the Exodus, as written \"and you shall tell your son on that day,\" etc.; berakha of redemption on the second wine cup; second netilat yadayim for the meal; berakha of netilat yadayim; ha-motzi and eating matzah; rabbinic mitzvah of eating matzah as written \"in the evening you shall eat matzot\"; eating maror to remember Egyptian bitterness and dipping in charoset in remembrance of clay; korekh matzah and maror together per Hillel the Elder; eating afikoman in remembrance of the Pesach offering; final hand-washing; rabbinic Birkat HaMazon; also saying on the third wine cup; mitzvah of reading Hallel and drinking the fourth wine cup — to repair the root of all these mitzvot in the supernal place through the hidden and concealed. And may the pleasantness, etc.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|4:א": "<b>(4) There. And he makes kiddush on it.</b> See above siman 472 s\"k 51 that we wrote the first cup corresponds to chochmah of binah — see there; on the first cup intend that the cup itself is gematria Elohim; nevertheless intend its exchanged aspect (meaning the letters of Elohim exchange with preceding letters per some, and some say they do not exchange) — the name AKDaT\"M; the five fingers of the right hand correspond to the five letters AKDaT\"M; wine in the cup also hints AKDaT\"M, for wine is gematria 70, and with the five fingers holding it behold 75, count of AKDaT\"M with the kollel; in the havayah of the kiddush berakha intend the name 72, aspect of chochmah.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|4:ב": "<b>(4) And the second cup</b> is aspect of binah of binah; intend in it the name AKDa\"M divided into two mems gimel lamed (meaning AKDa\"M is gematria gimel lamed); in the havayah of Asher Ga'alanu intend the name 63, aspect of binah.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|4:ג": "<b>(4) And the two</b> final cups are in the aspects of chesed and gevurah of da'at; therefore between the third and fourth one should not drink, so as not to separate them. The first cup is chesed of da'at — intend AKDa\"M in its calculation, gematria 74, with kollel 75, which is also yud beit kuf (meaning threefold havayah Elohim, each gematria yud beit kuf); and the name 45, aspect of chesed of da'at. The last cup is gevurot of da'at — intend AKDa\"M in its calculation gematria 74 and the name 52, aspect of gevurot of da'at. You should join both intentions in the first cup, then return to intend both in the second cup, as explained in Sha'ar HaKavanot and Pri Etz Chayim chapters 2–3 — see there.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|5:א": "<b>(5) There. And he makes kiddush on it.</b> He sanctifies standing — Seder HaYom; we are not accustomed thus unless Pesach is Shabbat because of Vaykhulu — as widespread in Hagahot, and so in Pesach Ma'uvin s\"k 208; however per Arizal all kiddushim must be standing, Shabbat and Yom Tov, as above siman 471 s\"k 62 — see there; and so Yafeh Lalev vol. 2 s\"k 1: he begins Eileh mo'adei Hashem, etc.; if on weekday — see there; meaning: but if on Shabbat, first say Yom ha-shishi Vaykhulu, etc., then Eileh mo'adei Hashem, etc., then Savri maranan, then wine berakha and kiddush and sheheheyanu; and before all say Mizmor le-David Hashem ro'i, etc., as above siman 471 s\"k 63 — see there.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|5:ב": "<b>(5) And know</b> there is no Torah-ordained Yom Tov kiddush, as above siman 471 s\"k 6 — see there; and further see above siman 472 s\"k 83.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|6:_": "<b>(6) There. And he recites sheheheyanu, etc.</b> With this he is exempt from reciting sheheheyanu on matzah — Mahari Molcho in responsum 36; Rabbeinu Yerucham s\"k 1; therefore when saying sheheheyanu on kiddush, intend to exempt the mitzvah of eating matzah; see below siman 643 seif 1.",
  "kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|7:_": "<b>(7) And that</b> one does not recite ha-tov ve-ha-metiv on the festival — because this good is not shared with another; it is comparable to rain fell and he has no field in partnership — investigate siman 221 that I do not recite — Mahari Molcho responsum 36 siman 101; Birkei Yosef s\"k 2; and Leket Yosher vol. 1 siman 58 wrote: in place of ha-tov ve-ha-metiv they enacted tefillat and in kiddush \"You chose us,\" etc. — there is no greater good — see there.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|1:_": "<b>(s\"k 1) Sheheheyanu, etc.</b> It should read: even if he blessed, etc., and did not bless on the second Yom Tov, etc.; Kaf HaChayim wrote: but he did not bless on the first Yom Tov night and blessed on the second night — he fulfills necessarily: if the first Yom Tov was weekday he certainly need not bless sheheheyanu except the second night; and if the first was Yom Tov, he completed with what he blessed on the second night — since the first was Yom Tov, the second weekday, he need not bless sheheheyanu on the second night for the second night; and the berakha he blessed on the second night ascends to complete the first day.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|2:א": "<b>(s\"k 2) And afterward, etc.</b> Since there is a dispute in siman 499 whether one who makes havdalah during the meal must recite borei peri hagafen on the havdalah cup.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|2:ב": "And if he did not have in mind to drink wine during the meal — in such a case all agree he must recite on every wine he drinks during the meal, all the more so the havdalah cup.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|2:ג": "<b>And one should not say havdalah and Hallel on one cup.</b> To say Birkat HaMazon and havdalah on one cup is explicit in the Gemara daf 102 — forbidden because two sanctifications on one cup appear as if the mitzvot are burdens on it; Magen Avraham added the same for Hallel and Hagadah — why on one cup?",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|2:ד": "<b>And if he forgot, etc.</b> he says Hallel and havdalah on one cup; and likewise if he remembered during Birkat HaMazon that he did not make havdalah, he recites Birkat HaMazon and havdalah on one — what is the difference? Rather since Magen Avraham deals with whether to say Hallel and havdalah on one cup, he also cites here Hallel and havdalah.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|3:א": "<b>(s\"k 3) And one should not, etc.</b> It implies he holds it forbidden, etc.; since he wrote \"but all year it appears,\" etc., he had to give a reason if one washes before kiddush — it implies preferable if one washes after kiddush; in siman 471 there is dispute among poskim on this.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|3:ב": "<b>And the reason the Gemara explains, etc.</b> He abbreviated greatly the reason of Beit Shammai, who hold it forbidden to use a vessel whose back is impure though its inside is pure, for the reason explained there; therefore Beit Shammai hold one must wash before pouring — if he pours first, perhaps sparks at pouring will fall on the back of the cup and receive impurity from hands that are impure before washing — they are second [degree], and the liquids become first [degree] and then defile the back of the vessel; though a vessel does not receive impurity except from a father of impurity, nevertheless rabbinically a vessel receives impurity from impure liquids even if the liquids are only first — therefore he washes hands first so they be pure. Beit Hillel hold it is permitted to use a vessel whose back is impure; therefore one must pour first — if he washes first, perhaps at pouring his hands are not dry and water is \"touching in order to touch\"; perhaps the vessel's back is impure and liquids on his hands receive impurity from the back and return to defile the hands. And further, even for Beit Shammai, Beit Hillel hold even if forbidden to use a vessel whose back is impure, preferable to pour first so pouring not be an interruption between washing and ha-motzi — thus far the Gemara.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|3:ג": "<b>Therefore it appears to me, etc.</b> Meaning: Maharil's intent is not as Darchei Moshe understood — Maharil brought in Sefer Avraham Rokeach: if one washes before kiddush it appears the law is like Beit Shammai who washes and then sanctifies — end of his words. If so, Maharil does not mean Beit Shammai require washing before kiddush; rather Beit Shammai must hold one need not be concerned for interruption between washing and ha-motzi — otherwise he would agree with Beit Hillel to pour first, for Beit Shammai's concern of back-impurity is only a slight concern and interruption is certain; or Beit Shammai hold even if interrupting between washing and ha-motzi it does not matter; but we hold like Beit Hillel that interruption is forbidden — nevertheless all year one who washes before kiddush has no interruption concern, for kiddush is not an interruption — only pouring is interruption, as Tosafot brought Magen Avraham in siman 166 that they poured in hot water; care needed not to omit or add water — see there; but on Pesach there is great interruption between washing and ha-motzi in Hagadah — certainly an interruption like pouring; and although immediately after kiddush he needs washing for dipping, and washing before kiddush could rise for dipping — nevertheless people will not understand he washes for dipping, since all year they do not wash for things dipped in liquid; they will err that he washes for ha-motzi and this is interruption like pouring; they will think the law is like Beit Shammai who are not concerned for interruption — such is Maharil's intent per Magen Avraham; and what Magen Avraham concluded \"and all agree on this\" — even Beit Shammai who hold pouring is not interruption nevertheless agree Hagadah and Hallel are interruption; and what Maharil wrote \"then it appears like Beit Shammai\" — even if for Beit Shammai it is interruption, one may say people will err that Hagadah interruption is only like pouring interruption, and as pouring is not interruption for Beit Shammai, so Hagadah.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|3:ד": "<b>And Maharshal, etc.</b> before kiddush so as not to change from all year.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|3:ה": "Bach wrote: nevertheless wash again for dipping, for he holds kiddush is an interruption in the complete sense between washing and ha-motzi — not that kiddush is interruption, since kiddush requires a meal — there is no kiddush except where there is a meal — they are considered one; unlike dipping, which has no need for kiddush — kiddush is the interruption.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|3:ו": "If so it appears contradictory; therefore if he washes before kiddush it is not evident he washes for dipping, only as all year when one washes before kiddush even though afterward he washes again for ha-motzi — nevertheless it is not so evident; see Taz s\"k 6 and Kaf HaChayim.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|4:א": "<b>(s\"k 4) And karpas, etc.</b> arduous labor; I heard from a great man who knew through investigation in medical books that karpas is what we call celery — through meat they improve; but if cooked alone without meat it would change for the worse; at least they would not improve — nevertheless one recites on it raw borei peri ha-adamah, as siman 200.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|4:ב": "I would say, meaning: even if surprising that even without meat they improve through cooking, nevertheless since in any case it is also eaten raw, one recites when raw borei peri ha-adamah.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|4:ג": "There is dispute — Tur and those who disagree; also one authority is unclear to me in Magen Avraham — apparently Maharil's father holds and doubts whether cooked is preferable even cooked without meat; for on maror too one recites borei peri ha-adamah — from the law in siman 475 seif 2: if he lacks vegetables for first dipping he takes maror and recites borei peri ha-adamah — see there.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|5:_": "<b>(s\"k 5) Salt water, etc.</b> for that meal it is permitted; nevertheless forbidden to put two-thirds salt and one-third water — see there.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|1:א": "<b>Sheheheyanu.</b> And if he did not bless on the first night he blesses all seven; and the same for every Yom Tov (Eruvin daf 40); and it appears to me that even if he blessed on the first and did not bless on the second day of Yom Tov of the diaspora, he blesses all seven.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|1:ב": "<b>And afterward he makes havdalah.</b> If he remembered in the middle of his meal that he had not made havdalah, he makes havdalah in the middle of his meal on the cup; and if he had in mind to drink wine during the meal he does not recite borei peri hagafen, since there is a dispute — doubtful berakhot to be lenient — see siman 499 s\"k 3; if he did not have in mind he must bless and should not wait until after the meal, for then it is like adding to the cups; and one should not say havdalah and Hallel on one cup, for two sanctifications on one cup are impossible — only kiddush and havdalah are permitted, since both are one matter, as Pesachim says: also in havdalah we say \"who distinguishes between holy and holy\"; if he forgot havdalah until after Birkat HaMazon he says Hallel and havdalah on one cup, for it is impossible otherwise, as siman 494 s\"k 3.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|1:ג": "<b>And one should not wash the hands, etc.</b> For if they wash before kiddush it implies they act like Beit Shammai, who hold kiddush requires washing; but all year it appears he washes for the meal, and although here too dipping after kiddush requires washing, nevertheless it should not enter people's minds that he washes for dipping and they think he washes for kiddush — end of Darchei Moshe in Maharil's name, who implies it is forbidden to wash oneself before kiddush (and see Orach Chayim 12); nevertheless I do not know where we find Beit Shammai hold kiddush requires washing — only in Berakhot chapter 8 they dispute: Beit Shammai: wash hands then pour the cup; Beit Hillel: pour then wash; the Gemara explains because of impurity and purity for Beit Hillel, because of interruption between washing and ha-motzi — therefore it appears we hold like Beit Hillel, and only pouring is interruption as Tosafot Arvei Pesachim, but kiddush is not interruption; on Pesach, where there is great interruption, they wash close to the meal — and all agree on this; Maharshal: take water before kiddush and wash hands, and not wash again for dipping; Bach: nevertheless wash again for dipping; I am not persuaded — as Maharshal, for all year we do not wash for dipping, so it appears contradictory — Abudraham siman 188.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|4:א": "<b>And karpas.</b> Maharil wrote: custom to take karpas, notarikon: sixty ten thousands did arduous labor; his father was unsure whether permitted to take parsley because it is better cooked than raw — then no borei peri ha-adamah, as siman 200 — end; I wonder why our countries are accustomed to take parsley; perhaps they hold through meat they improve, as written; and per what is written in the name of Tur — only when not eaten at all raw does one recite she-hakol; but when eaten raw, even if better cooked, nevertheless one recites when raw borei peri ha-adamah — if so, no difficulty; still my view wavers why they chose something disputed — he should take another vegetable; perhaps other vegetables are not so available on Pesach, or because in the Gemara vegetables are mentioned and other things are called lefet, and our vegetable is not eaten raw unless pickled, unavailable on Pesach; Tur and Gemara say one also recites on maror borei peri ha-adamah — maror there must have been fit to eat raw like latukh — see there.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|4:ב": "<b>Salt water.</b> Hagahot Maimoniot: if Pesach is Shabbat make salt water on erev Shabbat; but siman 321 s\"k 2: whatever is needed for that meal may be done on Shabbat; it appears after eating the vegetable it is permitted to remove vegetable and salt water from the plate.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|4:ג": "<b>Above everything.</b> Meaning: close to it.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|4:ד": "<b>Forearm (zeroa).</b> Ran wrote: bone without meat is not called a cooked dish; therefore a little meat should be attached (Darchei Moshe).",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|4:ה": "<b>On the coals.</b> For the Pesach was roasted only on a pomegranate skewer and trouble to return to it (Beit Yosef, Hagahot Maimoniot); forbidden to eat, for one does not eat roasted on Pesach nights; therefore forbidden to roast it on Yom Tov, for Yom Tov does not prepare for another day; it appears if he forgot to roast until night he roasts at night and eats it morning of the first Yom Tov; Maharshal wrote to cook a piece of meat and divide among those reclining; so Bach and Shlah; if he does not wish to eat the egg until the second night, likewise forbidden to roast it the first night (Darchei Moshe, Mahari\"v) — see 503.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|5:א": "<b>Chazeret.</b> Latukh; Levush wrote it is called in Polish zarzich.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|5:ב": "<b>Endives (olashin).</b> Korvil; krishfla in Arabic; andibi in Spanish (Hagahot Maimoniot).",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|5:ג": "<b>Tamcha.</b> Krien; in foreign tongue maertikh; Maharil was unsure whether korvil is maror; in foreign tongue marubya; charchevina: palm shoots.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|5:ד": "<b>Not from the root.</b> Mahari\"v: if taking maertikh, take what is above ground, leaves and adjacent parts, not below ground, which is root — end; so Mishna Berurah and Shlah; but earlier Mahari\"v wrote in first chapter of Sukkah: they said hyssop three stalks with three pods — Rashi: stalks are roots; from this it appears what Rabbenu Tam wrote \"not roots\" means small roots branching, but the large root the vegetable stands in is included in stalk — end; Hagahot Maimoniot: I heard stalk is called until below its tail and small spikes emerge — end; and so is essential: the world recites on leaves and for korekh takes the stalk; it appears the reason: since it says one fulfills with its stalk, leaves are preferable — therefore leaves for berakha; nevertheless need a kezayit, siman 486; because leaves are not so plentiful, take stalk for korekh; but if he wishes leaves for korekh, permission is his.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|5:ה": "<b>Whether moist.</b> It implies one does not fulfill with wilted; so Tur and Rambam — see Gemara.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|5:ו": "<b>Not pickled.</b> Rashi: in vinegar; it appears he follows his view as Beit Yosef Yoreh De'ah 105; but we hold something in water from the eve is pickled like cooked — if maror soaked from the eve one does not fulfill, bitterness nullified; see Beit Yosef siman 527 — Mahari\"v: Maharar\"a was unsure — since maror is compared to matzah and stolen is invalid, perhaps a Jew should not uproot maror from the ground, siman 649; it appears not comparable — there lettuce is sustained in earth therefore like earth — end; it appears since maror is not sustained in earth it is like movable property, gentile acquisition through despair and change of domain, change of domain in Israel's hand; still difficult, for we hold whatever is attached to ground is like ground, siman 637 s\"k 3, especially rooted in ground Yoreh De'ah 6:2 and Choshen Mishpat 95 and 193; perhaps when maror was already fully cooked and stands to be uprooted, he holds whatever stands to be uprooted is considered uprooted.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|5:ז": "<b>Or another bitter vegetable.</b> Shibolei HaLeket: Gemara proves one fulfills only with those enumerated; what Agur wrote to take wormwood — he explains maror is wormwood; if so, other bitter vegetables he eats without berakha — the Mechaber's words require study — end; it appears the truth is Agur's intent, for Agur wrote so in Maharil's name: one without latugi — some say the earlier in the mishnah is earlier for the mitzvah; there the four species are mentioned in foreign tongue (as above); Tashbetz: take vermet in foreign tongue, maror of the mishnah; reason: some reverse the order — therefore if no chazeret, for all maror is preferable; Agudah: take maertikh, tamcha; perhaps because olashin/korvil was not clarified — siddurim say korvil for first dipping, implying not maror — thus Maharil and Agudah; also maror is vermet; known wormwood is vermet in Ashkenaz; in Minhagei Maharik where no latugi they take wormwood called verimda or other bitter thing — Tashbetz — end; if so, maror of the mishnah includes all bitter vegetables; so Rashi in Chumash: any bitter herb is maror; but Gemara does not imply so; also Sukkah 13: even field bitterness one does not fulfill because it has a qualifying name — must be called maror without qualifier; also Rashi's intent seems all are called maror, nevertheless one fulfills only those called maror at matan Torah; therefore no berakha on other vegetables.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|5:ח": "<b>A little vinegar.</b> If Shabbat, put liquid from erev Shabbat (Agudah) — see 321:16: through change permitted to put liquid on Shabbat — put liquid first then charoset.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|5:ט": "<b>Similar to straw.</b> Not ground well and they are long (Beit Yosef, Rokeach); Maharil: do not grind them — they should be long.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|6:א": "<b>Less than a kezayit.</b> For if he eats a kezayit there is doubt whether he recites a concluding berakha (Tur); therefore if he happened to eat a kezayit he does not recite a concluding berakha.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|6:ב": "<b>And he recites borei peri ha-adamah.</b> Before dipping so it precede the act; the same for maror (Maharil); difficulty — the berakha refers only to eating, borei peri ha-adamah, and so for maror — requires study.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|6:ג": "<b>And he breaks it in two.</b> For it requires lechem oni — a poor person's way is with a piece.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|6:ד": "<b>And he gives half.</b> Meaning: the larger half (Maharil, Bach).",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|6:ה": "<b>Under the cloth.</b> Some place it on their shoulders in remembrance of the Exodus.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|6:ו": "<b>And he lifts the plate.</b> And need not remove the cooked dishes (Tur).",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|6:ז": "<b>Ha Lachma Anya.</b> Biblical language, as written \"so they will see the bread that I fed them,\" etc.; do not corrupt the formula to k'Ha Lachma or Ha Kolachma (Mahariv, Darchei Moshe, Mateh Moshe) — some do not want Ha Lachma because it is not the very bread Israel ate; proof from scripture \"the bread that I fed\" — yet that bread they already ate, only a sample of bread; here we bake new matzot — not fit to say Ha Lachma; therefore one who says Ha Kolachma or k'Ha Lachma did not lose.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|6:ח": "<b>And he commands to remove them.</b> Now they are not accustomed, for children lack recognition — they know these were not placed to eat; in Gemara times they were accustomed to clear the table.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|6:ט": "<b>And they will ask.</b> Tell them they may not eat until the Exodus is told; therefore no matzah on the table until after the seder (Maharil).",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|7:א": "<b>They pour, etc.</b> Do not say \"we are obligated to dip,\" etc. — it is only recognition for children; say \"we dip\" (Tur); when saying \"and it stood for our fathers,\" etc., hold the cup and cover bread until \"Hashem saves us from them\" (Shlah); when saying Pesach do not raise the meat; read venomar lefanav shirah chadashah haleluyah — on past redemption; some say Hallel was said by Moshe and Israel as in commentary Arvei Pesachim (Bach, Maharshal, Shlah).",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|7:ב": "<b>With his finger.</b> Custom to sprinkle with the finger — see there \"it is the finger of Hashem\" — unlike Hagahot Maimoniot to sprinkle with the small pinky — end Darchei Moshe; Yalkut parashat Va'era brings Perkei Rabi Eliezer: with the fist He struck Egypt — on this \"finger of Hashem\"; therefore sprinkle with the fist.",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|7:ג": "<b>Sixteen times.</b> Corresponding to the sword of the Holy One, blessed be He, called yud-heh-vav-heh (Darchei Moshe).",
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt|7:ד": "<b>He covers the bread.</b> Lest he see his nakedness, as siman 471:9; when Pesach is first day of Yom Tov say from the Pesach offerings and from the sacrifices — they did not offer the festival offering, for festival offering does not override Shabbat (Bach, Mahari\"v) — see Tur.",
  "mechaber/part-001.txt|1:main": "<b>Laws of the first cup and the Pesach seder until the second cup. Containing 7 seifim:</b><br>(1) They pour for him the first cup (1) and make kiddush on it (1) and recite (2)(1)(2)(1) sheheheyanu (3) and if it falls on (2)(2) Shabbat he says Vaykhulu, and if on Motza'ei Shabbat (4)(3) he says yaknehaz (3) and if he forgot havdalah and did not remember until he began the Hagadah (5)(4) he completes the Hagadah until Ga'al Yisrael (2)(6)(5) and afterward (3) makes havdalah: {Rama: (1)(7)(6)(7)(8) And one should not wash the hands at all before (4) kiddush (Rabbi Yerucham and the Rash in responsum and Mordekhai chapter Arvei Pesachim) and if his hands are not clean (7) he washes a little (8) but does not recite on the washing; and the master of the house (8) should not pour for himself (9) — only another pours for him in the manner of freedom (Mahariv)}",
  "mechaber/part-001.txt|2:main": "(4)(9)(10) He drinks reclining (5)(2)(11) and does not recite a berakha after it.",
  "mechaber/part-001.txt|3:main": "(6)(12) If he wishes to drink several cups (10)(13) permission is in his hand (7)(14) nevertheless (15) it is fit to be careful (16) not to drink between the first and second unless for great need (11)(17) so he not become intoxicated and be prevented from performing the seder and reading the Hagadah.",
  "mechaber/part-001.txt|4:main": "(8)(18) They bring before the master of the house a plate (19) containing three matzot and maror (20) and charoset (4)(12) and karpas (21) or another vegetable (5) (and vinegar (3)(13)(22) or (5)(6) salt water) (9)(1) and two cooked dishes, one in remembrance of the Pesach offering and one in remembrance of the festival offering; custom: meat and egg: {Rama: (23) And he arranges the plate before him so he need not pass over the matzot — karpas on top of all, vinegar closer than matzah, matzot before maror and charoset, and they closer than meat and egg (Maharil); custom: meat is the forearm (zeroa); meat roasted on coals, egg cooked (and likewise roasted — so in our city)}",
  "mechaber/part-001.txt|5:main": "<b>These are the vegetables with which one fulfills his obligation:</b><br>(11)(2)(33) chazeret (10) endives (11) tamcha charchevina (34)(meaning species of bitter herbs) (35) maror — one fulfills with their leaves and with their stalks (12)(5)(36) but not with the root (13) only that with leaves one does not fulfill (19)(37) unless they are moist, and with stalks one fulfills (13) whether moist or dry (14)(20)(38) but (14) not pickled (39) nor scalded nor cooked (40) and all (21) combine (13)(41) to a kezayit, which is their measure (15)(22)(42) and the essential mitzvah (43) is with chazeret; if he lacks chazeret (16)(44) he returns after first to first according to the order they are listed: {Rama: (45) if he lacks (46) one of these vegetables (47) he takes (23) wormwood (15)(24)(48) or another bitter (14) vegetable (Agur) (25) and charoset (49) he makes thick in remembrance of clay and afterward they put in it (16)(26)(50) a little (15) vinegar or red wine in remembrance of blood (Tur) and they make charoset from fruits with which Israel were compared (Tosafot chapter Arvei Pesachim) (51) such as apples, figs, nuts, pomegranates, almonds, and they put on it spices such as cinnamon and ginger (17)(27)(52) similar (16) to straw with which they kneaded the clay (Tur)}",
  "mechaber/part-001.txt|6:main": "(18)(41)(7)(42)(8)(43) He washes hands for first dipping (44)(18)(45) and does not recite on washing (46)(9)(47)(18)(48) and takes from karpas (19)(8)(18)(49) less than a kezayit (50) and dips in vinegar (19)(51) and recites borei peri ha-adamah and eats (29)(52) and does not recite after it; takes the middle matzah (20)(53)(20)(30)(54) and breaks it in two (21)(55) and gives half to one of those reclining to guard for afikoman (19)(56) and they place it under the cloth (22)(57) and the other half between the two whole ones (21)(23)(31)(58) and lifts the plate (22)(59) with the matzot and says (32)(24)(60) Ha Lachma Anya until Mah Nishtanah: {Rama: (62)(63) say in a language (2)(64) women and children understand, or explain; R' Yitzchak of London did entire Hagadah in vernacular (Kol Bo and Mahariv)} (23)(25)(33) then commands (26) to remove them from the table (65) and place at the end as if already eaten so children see (26)(66) and ask:",
  "mechaber/part-001.txt|7:main": "(24)(27)(67)(34) Immediately pour the second cup (68)(69) so children ask why a second cup before the meal; if the son lacks wisdom the father teaches; if no son, his wife asks; if not, he asks himself; even scholars ask one another (9)(24)(35) Mah Nishtanah, etc. (70)(when son or wife asks, need not say Mah Nishtanah but begin Avadim) (Maharil) (25)(71) when beginning Avadim, i.e., to Pharaoh, return the plate with matzot and read all Hagadah (26)(72) when reaching \"this matzah\" raise to show (small: raised broken matzah, lechem oni) (Mahari\"v) (36)(73) likewise \"this maror\" (27)(74) when reaching Lefichak each raises his cup until concluding Ga'al Yisrael: {Rama: custom to sprinkle a little from the cup (28)(37)(26)(75) with his finger (opinion) at blood, fire, smoke; likewise plagues dtzach adash b'achav (28)(76) sixteen times (Maharil) (77) bread uncovered (38)(39) while saying Hagadah until Lefichak (40)(27)(78) when holding cup (79) then cover bread (30)(Agur and Beit Yosef)}",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|1:א": "(1) And he makes kiddush on it — before kiddush intend to fulfill kiddush and four cups, kiddush cup being one of four; some say \"I am ready to sanctify and fulfill four cups\" — careful not to say this after kiddush since he already blessed borei peri hagafen on the cup, only before kiddush; before Hagadah intend or say to fulfill telling the Exodus [Chayei Adam]. Sheheheyanu — before drinking; if not before drinking, bless whenever he remembers day one, even in the market, time is rolling; if remembered on second night after sanctifying the day, exempt with sheheheyanu on the cup after kiddush; if forgot sheheheyanu on second night's kiddush, even if already blessed first night, obligated whenever remembers through the festival until end of last diaspora Yom Tov; likewise other Yom Tov until festival end.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|1:ב": "(2) On Shabbat — permitted to say Hagadah from a book even alone; we are not concerned he may tilt, as explained above siman 475:9 — see there.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|1:ג": "(3) He says yaknehaz — meaning: first wine berakha and kiddush and ner, i.e., borei me'orei ha-esh and havdalah and sheheheyanu; do not bless on spices every Motza'ei Shabbat to Yom Tov; say havdalah seated like kiddush.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|1:ד": "(4) He completes, etc. — to make havdalah immediately when remembered he cannot, for havdalah requires a cup and the one who blesses must taste; during Hagadah forbidden to interrupt with drinking; from the Mechaber's wording \"until he began Hagadah\" implies if remembered before Hagadah he need not wait but immediately pours a cup and says havdalah berakha and drinks — however this is only per the Mechaber who does not bless on each cup; per Rama 474 gloss and our custom to bless each cup, he must bless borei peri hagafen — then it appears like adding cups, forbidden, as siman 472:7; therefore possibly even if remembered before Hagadah there is no remedy until completing Hagadah, unless at time of first cup blessing he will drink between cups as siman 472:7 — see Bi'ur Halakha. If forgot Hagadah at night — no compensation, for \"because of this\" when matzah and maror before you; if forgot kiddush at night — law as siman 471:8.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|1:ה": "(5) And afterward makes havdalah — bless borei peri hagafen on second cup and also ner and Yom Tov havdalah and drink; if remembered mid-meal he did not make havdalah, must interrupt immediately and make havdalah, not borei peri hagafen, for second cup drunk before eating exempts unless he then had no intent to drink during eating — then also borei peri hagafen; if remembered during Birkat HaMazon — Birkat HaMazon and havdalah on one cup; if not until after cup of Birkat HaMazon — wait until after Hallel and Hagadah, then havdalah on fourth cup; if not until after fourth — havdalah on fifth cup and must bless borei peri hagafen since he already removed his mind from drinking [later authorities].",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|1:ו": "(6) And one should not wash, etc. — meaning: even those who all year wash before kiddush for the meal — for them kiddush is not interruption between washing and meal as explained siman 471 — nevertheless on Pesach night, where they interrupt much after kiddush in Hagadah and lose attention to guarding hands, all agree not to do so; even to wash so as not to need wash again after kiddush for karpas is also not proper per later authorities.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|1:ז": "(7) He washes a little — wrote \"a little\" to heed the reason Darchei Moshe brought: do not wash before kiddush lest it appear kiddush requires washing — therefore not a full washing.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|1:ח": "(8) But do not bless, etc. — even if full washing, careful not to bless al netilat yadayim, for this washing does not count for eating — berakha in vain.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|1:ט": "(9) Only another pours for him — if possible.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|2:א": "(10) He drinks reclining — if forgot and did not recline, see siman 472:7 and Mishna Berurah s\"k 21.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|2:ב": "(11) And does not bless after it — on the vine, etc., even if drank a revi'it — relies on Birkat HaMazon at end; also will recite concluding berakha after four cups — explained well siman 474.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|3:א": "(12) Several cups — whether between first and second or after second cup, even during eating; but between third and fourth forbidden, as below siman 479.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|3:ב": "(13) Permission is in his hand — per Mechaber below 474 gloss: our custom first berakha on each cup — permitted to drink between cups only if at berakha time he intended to exempt all he would later drink [or wine was before him, automatically exempt if he did not explicitly decide not to drink]; if no intent to drink between cups and later decided to drink — must bless; forbidden, appears like adding cups since he blessed this cup like the four — as written siman 472 s\"k 21 in Mishna Berurah.",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|3:ג": "(14) It is fitting to be careful — and such is our custom [Darkei Moshe]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|3:ד": "(15) Not to drink between the first and second [cup] — to be concerned for the opinion of someone who says (and it is brought in Beit Yosef) that what we permit to drink between the cups is only between cup 2 and cup 3, which is close to the meal or during the meal, for then one does not become intoxicated; but not between the first and second, so that one should not become intoxicated, and as it is forbidden between the 3rd and 4th:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|3:ה": "(16) So that one should not become intoxicated — it implies specifically wine or other beverages of this kind that intoxicate; but a beverage that does not intoxicate, it is permitted to drink between the cups:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:א": "(17) They bring before the master of the house — but before the other members of his household they need not arrange in this order; rather all take from the master of the house's [portion]. This applies even in places where there is a small table before each one, and all the more so in our place where all sit on one table:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ב": "(18) That it has three matzos — two for lechem mishneh as on other Yom Tov, and one in order to break it into two: half of it to fulfill therewith the mitzvah of eating matzah, for the Merciful One calls it \"bread of affliction,\" and the way of a poor person is in a piece and a half; the second for afikoman, as explained below in seif 6:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ג": "(19) And charoset — in order to dip the maror therein, as below in siman 475. And karpas is a type of vegetable called karpas, and they chose l'chatchila this species because it is a notarikon: sixty [times ten thousand], meaning six hundred thousand performed hard labor:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ד": "(20) Or another vegetable — of any species; but l'chatchila it is good to take from that species over which one recites that berachah that one recites over maror, so that he should exempt through this the maror that he eats during the meal; but he should not take for this one of the five species of maror, for after he has filled his belly with maror how can he afterward bless on eating maror — and see below in siman 475 seif 2:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ה": "(21) Or salt water — to dip therein the karpas and the vegetable. If it falls on Shabbat he should make the salt water before Shabbat and not make it on Shabbat, as written in siman 321 seif 1; and if he did not make it before Shabbat and he has no vinegar to dip therein, salt water should be made in small quantity, as written there. And behold, the matter of dipping the vegetable in liquid is a rabbinic enactment, in order to astonish the children so they see a change — that they eat vegetables by dipping, which is not their manner to eat them before the meal throughout all the days of the year — and they will ask about this change, for the obligation of reciting the Haggadah is to say it as an answer to questions that were asked him, as it is said: \"when your son asks you,\" etc., \"and you shall say to your son: we were slaves,\" etc.:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ו": "(22) A remembrance of the festival offering — and if [the first day of Pesach] falls on Shabbat, some say one need take only one cooked dish corresponding to the Pesach, for the festival offering does not come then since it does not override Shabbat; and some say that nevertheless one must make two cooked dishes as in other years, and the Sages did not distinguish in this, since it is only a mere remembrance — and such is the custom:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ז": "(23) And they are accustomed with meat and egg — and all the more so if he wishes to make two types of meat, one roasted and one cooked, as a remembrance of the Pesach and the festival offering, for that is fine; rather that he also fulfills through the egg. And some wrote that what they chose an egg — meaning, the Merciful One seeks to redeem us. And some wrote because we make a remembrance of the mourning for the Temple, for we are unable to offer the Pesach sacrifice:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ח": "(24) In a manner that he does not need, etc. — meaning that he should not need to skip them:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ט": "(25) Above, etc. — meaning next to it:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:י": "(26) And they should be closer, etc. — and some wrote that one need not be strict about the meat and egg if he will need to skip them, since they are not a mitzvah but a mere remembrance; and also regarding maror and charoset it is possible that \"one does not pass over\" does not apply — and see in the Acharonim the order of the seder plate of the Ari z\"l:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:כ": "(27) Foreleg — on account of \"and I shall pass by/outstretched arm\" that Hashem showed in Egypt. And one who has no foreleg should take other meat, even without a bone [Peri Megadim]; the poskim wrote that we require there be a little meat on the foreleg, which is a remembrance of the meat of the Pesach sacrifice:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ל": "(28) It should be roasted — and not cooked, as a remembrance of the Pesach sacrifice:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:מ": "(29) On the coals — and even though the Pesach was ideally roasted on a spit, nevertheless since it was only roasted on a pomegranate-wood spit, which requires trouble to turn it around, therefore it suffices for us with roasting on coals, which fulfills from the essence of the law also for the Pesach sacrifice [Magen Avraham]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:נ": "(30) And the egg should be, etc. — since it is a remembrance of the festival offering, which does not require roasting:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ס": "(31) And the same applies if roasted — for the festival offering comes whether roasted or cooked:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|4:ע": "(32) And thus is our custom in our city — to roast the egg. And it is forbidden to eat the [meat of the] foreleg at night, for one does not eat roasted [meat] at night; but the egg, even though it is roasted, is permitted, for there is no prohibition of roasted [meat] on an egg; and therefore it is forbidden to roast the foreleg at night except m'b'eod yom (before nightfall), unless his intention is to eat it tomorrow on that day; and therefore if he forgot and roasted it at night, he should be careful to eat it tomorrow morning — so wrote Magen Avraham and other Acharonim; and if his intention is not to eat the egg until the second night, the egg too is forbidden to roast at night except m'b'eod yom. Chayei Adam wrote: evil is this practice that they throw away the foreleg, and it is disgrace to a mitzvah. And it is a mitzvah to leave [it] on the second Yom Tov morning into the roast that they roast for Yom Tov, and they eat it:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:א": "(33) For his obligation — meaning for the obligation of the mitzvah of maror, to fulfill what is written: \"upon matzos and bitter herbs shall they eat it\"; and now that we have no Pesach, matzah and maror are only d'rabbanan. Just as one does not fulfill with stolen matzah, as above in siman 454, so one does not fulfill with stolen maror; and therefore one should be careful that a Jew should not uproot the maror himself from a gentile's land, even if he gives him permission, for generally gentiles are land-thieves; and abandonment does not apply to land, as below in siman 649 seif 1 in the Hagah — rather the gentile should uproot it himself, and then abandonment applies there, and afterward the Jew acquires it from him, for there is through this abandonment and change of ownership:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ב": "(34) Meaning: types of bitter herbs — all of them are referred to; and because we do not know clearly which they are in our language, therefore he wrote in general \"all of them,\" that they are bitter species; and the Acharonim wrote that tamcha is chrain in our language, and on chazeret wrote Chayei Adam, and likewise Chafetz Chaim which is what we call salatin:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ג": "(35) Maror — it is also a species of bitter vegetable known to them by the name maror, on account of that it is very bitter:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ד": "(36) But not in the root — meaning: small roots that branch to here and to there at its base or at its sides; but the large root in which the vegetable stands is the stalk. And behold, in our countries the other species are not found, and therefore the maror customary in our country to eat is tamcha (and it is chrain); and one should be careful not to eat it when it is whole, for it is nearly a danger and there is no mitzvah in it, for because of its sharpness it is greatly harmful; therefore one must grate it on a grater and leave it thus exposed, and then it mitigates its sharpness. And the Gra instituted not to grate it before his coming from the synagogue, so that he should not mitigate the taste, for then one does not fulfill with it; rather he should grate it after his arrival and cover it until the beginning of the seder [and when Pesach falls on Shabbat he should grate it m'b'eod yom and place it in a vessel and cover it until the beginning of the seder] and then he should scatter it on the plate and thereby mitigate its sharpness; and then he should crush it and estimate that there should be a k'zayit, and he can eat it properly:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ה": "(37) Unless they are moist — dried ones have no taste of maror and behold they are as mere dust; not so in the stalk, for since it is thick, even if it is dry it does not lose its taste. And wilted leaves — some say one fulfills with them, and some are strict even with wilted ones:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ו": "(38) But not pickled — that is, if he soaked them in water from the outset; and some say specifically if he pickled them in vinegar; and therefore l'chatchila one should be careful not to soak the chrain in water from the outset, and b'dieved one may rely to be lenient when he has no others, because in chrain we see that there is still in it taste of maror. And it appears to me that with leaves one should not be lenient, for possibly through pickling from the outset in water the taste of maror emerges in them. And know that one fulfills with leaves even l'chatchila. And behold, I have seen many weak people who press themselves to fulfill the mitzvah of maror with stalks, and I do not know why they do not take the leaves for the mitzvah — perhaps because dry and wilted ones are commonly found in them. However, if they are moist, one should not be strict in this at all:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ז": "(39) And not boiled/softened — greatly boiled and boiled in the manner of cooking — all because they have no taste of maror through this:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ח": "(40) And all of them, etc. — meaning all five species mentioned; and we do not say that since every vegetable certainly has a bitter taste by itself they do not combine, for the taste of one is nullified in its fellow — it teaches us that since in any case all of them have a taste of bitterness, they combine [Ran]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ט": "(41) To a k'zayit — it is the measure of half an egg (and see below siman 486); and if he fulfills the mitzvah with leaves, he should see to press them together, for the space between the leaves does not combine to a k'zayit; and one should be careful in this, for otherwise it is a berachah in vain, since he blesses on eating and eating implies a k'zayit; and also he does not fulfill therewith the mitzvah of maror:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:י": "(42) With chazeret — since it is a remembrance of Egyptian bondage, whose beginning was soft and at the end hard; and so too is the nature of chazeret, at first sweet and at the end bitter; and the Acharonim wrote that even if it is somewhat more expensive than other maror, it is also proper to seek it out; however they wrote that in the species chazeret (i.e. salatin) tiny worms are very common on Pesach days that are not recognizable to weak eyes; therefore one who does not have special God-fearing people to check it properly, it is better to take tamcha (which they call chrain), even though it is third in the order in which they are enumerated, for Heaven forbid to stumble in a prohibition on account of fulfilling a positive rabbinic commandment — and especially since both can be fulfilled through tamcha:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:כ": "(43) He should seek first first — and one who is ill or delicate is permitted to take whichever species is more pleasant to him; and he may also eat the k'zayit little by little within the measure of k'vei'achalat peras, for according to the essence of the law one fulfills thus, as below siman 475 seif 6; and if even so it is impossible for him because of his health, at minimum he should eat a little or chew in his mouth to remember the taste of bitterness, but he should not bless on this:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ל": "(44) One of these vegetables — meaning the five mentioned:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:מ": "(45) He should take wormwood — see in Biur Halachah:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:נ": "(46) Or other bitter vegetable — those that have the signs mentioned in the Gemara regarding maror, that it should be fit for eating, and that it has sap (meaning: when they cut it, at the place of the cut white sap comes out like milk), and its leaves are pale (meaning its leaf is not very green like the leaves of onions and other vegetables, but leans somewhat to whiteness); nevertheless regarding berachah one should not bless on them, because we are not so expert in these signs:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ס": "(47) He should make it thick — and make it m'eve yom tov; and if he forgot, it is permitted to make it on yom tov:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:ע": "(48) A little vinegar — of wine or red wine, in order to soften it and it shall be a remembrance of blood. If it falls on Shabbat he should put the liquid in it from erev Shabbat; and if he forgot, see above in siman 321 seif 16 and in Mishna Berurah there seif kaf 68:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:פ": "(49) Such as apples — on account of the verse \"under the apple tree I aroused you\"; and so all other fruits that are written in Shir HaShirim; and almonds on account that Hashem was diligent regarding the end to do [redemption]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|5:צ": "(50) Resembling straw — that they are not ground well and they are long:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:א": "(51) For the need of the first dipping — as he explains and continues. And the reason is that everything whose dipping is in liquid requires netilah, as above in siman 158:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ב": "(52) And he should not bless, etc. — as the Mechaber ruled above in siman 158 seif 1; and see there in Mishna Berurah the opinion of the Gra on this matter:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ג": "(53) Less than a k'zayit — because with a k'zayit there is doubt in the blessing acharonah whether he should bless it or not; therefore it is better that he eat less than a k'zayit so there should be no obligation at all according to all:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ד": "(54) In vinegar — or in wine or in salt water, and this comes only to exclude that he should not dip in charoset, for charoset is only for the second dipping when one dips the maror in charoset [Beit Yosef]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ה": "(55) And he blesses Borei peri ha'adamah — and he should intend to exempt through this berachah also the maror that he will eat afterward [Acharonim]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ו": "(56) And he does not bless after it — even if he ate a k'zayit, because the first berachah stands also for the maror, as above; and therefore the Grace after Meals that exempts the maror that he eats during the meal stands also for the karpas that he ate earlier — and see in Biur Halachah what he wrote in the name of the Gra on this:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ז": "(57) The middle one and he breaks it into two — in order to fulfill what the verse called \"bread of affliction,\" and the way of a poor person is in a piece; and he said \"the middle one\" because the berachah of eating matzah primarily applies to it, and the berachah of HaMotzi that he blesses at the beginning applies to the upper one placed before him:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ח": "(58) And he gives half, etc. — so that the two eatings that he eats for the sake of matzah should be in a piece; and it is good that that portion should be larger [Acharonim]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ט": "(59) Under the cloth — a remembrance of what is said \"their leftovers tied in their garments\"; and some place it on their shoulders as a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt. And they should be careful to wrap the afikoman in a cloth that was not laundered with k'rachma'al, for cloths are generally laundered with k'rachma'al [Chayei Adam]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:י": "(60) And he raises the plate — with everything that is on it, and he need not remove the cooked dishes:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:כ": "(61) Ha lachma anya — and some say k'ha lachma anya. And he must say it in a loud voice:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ל": "(62) And he says in the language, etc. — meaning from Ha lachma anya onward, which is the beginning of the Haggadah:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:מ": "(63) In a language that they understand, etc. — and he fulfills therewith what the verse said \"and you shall tell your son,\" etc.:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:נ": "(64) So that the women should understand — for behold women too are obligated in the mitzvos of the night and in reciting the Haggadah, as above in siman 472 seif 14; and therefore the obligation is also on the maidservant that she sit at the table and hear the entire Haggadah; and if they need to go outside to cook, at minimum she is obligated to hear Kiddush; and when he reaches \"Rabban Gamliel says,\" etc., she should enter and hear until after drinking cup 2, for one who did not say these three things has not fulfilled [his obligation]; and the custom is that they also read to them so they hear the order of the ten plagues that Hashem brought upon Egypt, in order to tell them how many miracles Hashem did for Israel [Chayei Adam]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ס": "(65) And to place them at the end of the table — and if the table is small, remove them entirely from the table, for otherwise there is no change for the child [Eliyah Rabbah]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|6:ע": "(66) And they will ask — the matzos prepared for eating: why do we remove them? And he says to them that they are not permitted to eat until he tells of the Exodus from Egypt. Maharil wrote that one should not place any matzah on the table until the time of the meal:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:א": "(67) Immediately — meaning that he should not wait in mixing the cup until he reaches \"therefore,\" for then he needs to hold the cup in his hand; rather immediately when he removes the plate from the table he mixes:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:ב": "(68) Second cup — and he need not rinse and wash, for they already rinsed for Kiddush:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:ג": "(69) So that they will ask, etc. — meaning and thereby he will be aroused to ask further questions and wonders that he sees on that night:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:ד": "(70) And when the son or the woman asks — and the same applies if one Torah scholar asks his fellow; the one asked need not say \"why is this night different\":",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:ה": "(71) And he reads the entire Haggadah — and one should not say it reclining, only in awe and fear [Shelah]. When he says \"and we shall say before Him song,\" it must be \"v'ne'emar\" with segol vowels under the alef, and \"ne'emar\" is past tense, for it refers to the redemption from Egypt [Acharonim]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:ו": "(72) And likewise when he reaches \"this maror\" — but when he reaches \"the Pesach that our forefathers,\" etc., he should not hold in his hand the meat that is a remembrance of the Pesach, for it is as raising consecrated [meat] outside [the Temple precincts], for it appears from this that he consecrated it for the Pesach. Mordechai wrote that one must say \"and we shall eat there of the sacrifices and of the Pesach offerings,\" for they eat first of the festival offering, which is the sacrifice; but the Pesach is eaten on the full [stomach]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:ז": "(73) And when he reaches \"therefore\" he raises, etc. — and in Shelah he wrote that also when he says the passage \"and it is this that stood for our forefathers and for us,\" etc., until \"Hashem saves us from their hand,\" he should hold the cup in his hand; and then the bread should be covered when he holds the cup in his hand:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:ח": "(74) With his finger — the custom is to sprinkle with the finger on account of \"it is the finger of God,\" not as written in Hagahat Minhagim to sprinkle with the little finger:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:ט": "(75) Sixteen times — corresponding to the letters yud-hey of the Name of Hashem with which He struck Pharaoh:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:י": "(76) And the bread should be uncovered, etc. — for therefore matzah is called \"bread of affliction,\" that one answers upon it matters:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:כ": "(77) That he holds the cup in his hand — in order to say song over the wine:",
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|7:ל": "(78) And then he covers the bread — so that his nakedness should not be seen, as is written siman 471 seif 9:",
  "netiv-chayim/part-001.txt|1:_": "<small>(Magen Avraham seif kaf 13)</small> It implies that one does not fulfill with wilted ones; Mishna Berurah: so wrote Riaz in the name of the Yerushalmi chapter 2 halachah 5 that leaves are invalid — and this is a very great stringency, for how will he act properly? If in water we are concerned for pickled; if he leaves it without water we are concerned for wilted — go out and see what the public does, for they are accustomed to fulfill with wilted ones. And in order to reconcile the custom I searched the source of the law in the Yerushalmi, and this is its language: \"between wilted\" (meaning it refers to the mishnah that reads \"between wilted\") — there is a baraita: two, but not wilted. Rav Chisda said: the one who says wilted [applies] to the stalk, and the one who says not wilted [applies] to the leaves, end of his words. And from this he ruled that one does not fulfill with wilted leaves. And behold, this is according to the text of the Yerushalmi that reads in the mishnah \"between wilted,\" which requires resolving the baraita so it should not contradict the mishnah; but according to our text that reads \"between dry ones,\" one can say the baraita comes according to one opinion and we rule like R' Meir and his colleagues and like Ra'avad, and so I found in Ra'avan page 73 that one fulfills with wilted ones. And further, since R' Eliezer said only in the stalk, but in leaves moist ones — not dry ones — from that R' Eliezer said \"dry\" and did not say \"wilted,\" it implies wilted ones are valid; the truth was to explain his words as Rashbam wrote in Pesachim page 101 s.v. lekiv'a kama, etc.; and that which he said \"moist\" is not precise, as the Rosh wrote in the chapter of stolen lulav siman 10 see there — and therefore the poskim omitted this law, for they did not need it: individual and many — the halachah is like the many, and examine:",
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt|1:_": "<b>And there is no</b> Taz: Beit Yosef understood that Mordechai refers to the first dipping — that if he washes hands before Kiddush and blesses al netilat yadayim, he will need at the first dipping of vegetables in salt water to wash again, and the berachah of al netilat yadayim of the first [washing] will be in vain because of the interruption; and therefore Beit Yosef wondered, for Kiddush alone is not an interruption as on other days of the year. And Taz explained that Mordechai refers to the second dipping — maror in charoset — and it is not specifically the second dipping but rather it refers to HaMotzi, for he needed to wash again and bless al netilat yadayim for HaMotzi, for the Haggadah is an interruption and the first berachah is in vain; and the language of the Gemara Pesachim 116b holds as Tosafot wrote there s.v. tzarich. And that which Mordechai wrote \"because he needs,\" etc. — emend and it is fitting that it should read: because for this reason he needs to wash hands at the second dipping even though he washed at the first dipping, for the Haggadah is an interruption; all the more so if he washes before Kiddush it is obvious there is interruption and he will need to wash again for the second dipping and for HaMotzi. Or one must emend and the reading is: even though if he washes before Kiddush he will still need to wash for dipping 2 and for HaMotzi, etc.; and see N'tziv who explained like Taz. And that which N'tziv wrote there that Mordechai holds for dipping in liquid one need not bless al netilat yadayim — one can explain likewise, but since Kiddush is an interruption on Pesach according to Beit Yosef's explanation in Mordechai, therefore berachah 1 of al netilat yadayim was in vain and by necessity he must afterward bless a second time al netilat yadayim; however that which Taz wrote plainly that the Haggadah is an interruption — one can explain: behold in siman 475 Beit Yosef at the beginning of the siman brought in the name of Mordechai that if from the outset he intended for this and did not divert his attention, he is permitted; Magen Avraham brought it there sign 1 and explains that which is written in the Gemara \"perhaps diverting attention\" means without intention, but if he intended for this and did not divert attention it is fine; if so, why is it different on Pesach from all days of the year — behold there is a remedy as written, and Eliyah Rabbah 475 and see there it will be explained. And see Levush in seif 1: he wrote if he washes before Kiddush he will need at the first dipping to wash and bless, and the second berachah will be in vain, see there; and he follows his view in seif 6 that even on other days of the year one does not bless al netilat yadayim for dipping in liquid; on Pesach one blesses al netilat yadayim for dipping in liquid (and the custom is not so even on Pesach, see below); and if so one should explain Mordechai thus, and N'tziv's question is not difficult either; and for the halachah: if he washed hands before Kiddush and blessed al netilat yadayim, it is obvious — whoever did not divert his attention during the Haggadah and did not touch an unclean place, it is forbidden to bless again al netilat yadayim for HaMotzi; and as a mere stringency he should wash hands for HaMotzi without berachah al netilat yadayim, for in the Gemara it says that according to one opinion \"diverting,\" etc.; and see siman 158 seif 7 in the Hagah and Taz there sign 11, that even when he washed hands for dipping in liquid and did not bless al netilat yadayim and did not intend that this washing should count for HaMotzi, nevertheless he should not bless afterward al netilat yadayim for HaMotzi as long as he did not divert his attention; and if so it is fitting for everyone when he washes hands for dipping 1 that he should intend explicitly not to fulfill with this washing for HaMotzi; and proof from what Rashal wrote Yoreh De'ah siman 3: even though non-sacred meat does not require intention, nevertheless if he intended to gouge and uproot the opposite of the intention, it is invalid — the same here; and see 198 siman 61 in niddah who went down to inspect: it appears at first glance that even intention not for immersion but only to inspect, immersion applies to her; and if so it is better that before HaMotzi he bring himself to obligation of washing by pouring on his feet or foot-water and rubbing, and bless al netilat yadayim, for whoever acts out of doubt does not bless a berachah that is not needed, and Eliyah Rabbah; and Magen Avraham 3 and Eliyah Rabbah there will explain further on this, see there:",
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt|2:א": "<b>And he does not</b> Taz: and for the first three cups b'dieved it suffices with the majority of a revi'it that he drinks his full mouthfuls; and a cup of revi'it requires that he drink a full revi'it in order to bless the blessing acharonah — see siman 190 in Taz 3 and 486 1:",
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt|2:ב": "<b>See</b> in Tur what the Rosh wrote: even if he diverted his attention from drinking, he drinks between the first two cups and it does not appear as adding to the cups — even a berachah is difficult from what the Rosh wrote. In Pesachim 108a between 3 and 4 it appears as adding, for according to him there is no distinction between berachah or not — Magen Avraham 472 sign 4; and some write that it is forbidden to drink between 3 and 4 and also after 4 from custom — see siman 481; it would be for the Rosh to omit this reason, which is the main one. And Bach explained that even for the Rosh, whatever he says in a berachah is adding, and without berachah and without Haggadah it is not adding — if so, it requires study:",
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt|3:_": "<b>Or</b> Taz: and see siman 321 seif 2 that two-thirds salt and one-third water — even a little is forbidden; and Taz there 6 and Magen Avraham here 5 and Eliyah Rabbah there will be explained:",
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt|4:_": "<b>They are accustomed</b> Taz: meat to roast on coals as a remembrance of the Pesach on a pomegranate spit; and because of the trouble they roast on coals, for also the Pesach if roasted on coals is fine — Eshel Avraham 5:280 chapter 8 halachah 10, Magen Avraham 8. And that which Taz wrote that one should not eat the meat on the first night — Pri Chadash agreed; and even if the meat is cooked, as long as it is a remembrance of the Pesach one does not eat on the first night except tomorrow if he wishes; and the egg is permitted to eat at night, for not only on account of remembrance of the festival offering do they cook an egg — there is also a reason of remembrance of mourning; and thereby he gave a reason for what the Mechaber wrote (Tur Rama): meat they roast and the egg they cook, because they are accustomed to eat the egg at night; if so, also fish and egg have no prohibition of roasted, as siman 476 seif 6; nevertheless they instituted on erev Yom Tov to cook the egg as well. And one can say for us: the festival offering of the 14th is optional and eaten cooked like all peace-offerings — Eshel Avraham 5:280 chapter 8 halachah 12 and Pesachim 70a; if so, the custom to cook the egg is a remembrance of the festival offering, etc. However Mishna Berurah wrote that they are accustomed to roast also the egg and one is permitted to eat the egg at night even roasted; but the meat, even cooked, they do not eat at night except by day tomorrow — see Eliyah Rabbah 86 seif 5: if they cooked a non-kosher egg with kosher eggs, see there; and wheat among them — 51 in Pesach forbids even m'b'eod yom until night if there are not sixty against the wheat, and if they are not peeled they are forbidden — see there Taz 10 and Shach 15:",
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt|5:_": "<b>But</b> Taz and Magen Avraham: maratich is (chrain); also what is beneath the ground comes out; and root means the small ones — see there:",
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt|6:_": "<b>He washes</b> Taz: and see siman 158 seif 2 and Eliyah Rabbah there, and he explains well sign 11 in the name of Mateh Yosef who was very strict. But one does not bless al netilat yadayim on something whose dipping is in liquid, even on the night of Pesach — and see Levush here and Eliyah Rabbah in 158. And know that there are three reasons: (1) on account of residue of terumah, and this is the opinion of Rashi z\"l — even though for fruits 43, nevertheless liquids are prone [to contract impurity] and become primary; they decreed more stringently; and therefore even a moist liquid also requires washing. And Tosafot Pesachim 116a s.v. kol: the reason is that it is forbidden to defile one's body; and therefore specifically when there is in the liquids a revi'it, the measure of defiling the body — Eshel Avraham, Taz chapter 4 halachah 2; and possibly the Sages did not distinguish. And Beit Yosef Orach Chaim 164 in the name of R' Yehudah: the reason is on account of cleanliness — Taz there sign 6; and in Sefer Rosh Yosef in Chullin we wrote: one who dips a radish in salt and it becomes water and dissolves — one can say its dipping is in liquid — Magen Avraham 462:7 and Kaf HaChayim seif 12 and Rambam z\"l chapter 8 halachah 49. And see Darkei Moshe in 158: plum juice, since it changed, they bless Borei peri ha'eitz — it is not \"something whose dipping is in liquid,\" and it requires study whether we say that for impurity it is a liquid, and likewise honey-water and beer of this kind; and that which Tosafot wrote that it is forbidden to defile one's body — one can see in Chullin 44 that it appears only a kohen is warned, and rabbinically from the Torah only for impurity of the dead, but an Israelite is not warned — see Chochmas Adam in four views. And Magen Avraham 489:4 — and it is simple that a kohen is warned rabbinically in the time of the Temple not to defile his body; nevertheless it is a mixture that is visible to Israelites. And behold on the night of Pesach, which implies in Levush that they bless al netilat yadayim for dipping in liquid — one can say that on the festival all Israelites are warned to purify themselves; and it is fine to say they are careful specifically on Yom Tov, and the same on every Yom Tov. Butter — Taz 164:9 and Magen Avraham 7, and so 81 and 89 from Taz: curdled milk — if we say it is not a liquid, butter too even though it dissolved. See in Pri Megadim in siman 158, see there:",
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt|7:_": "<b>And not</b> Taz. And we already wrote in the previous sign that one can say on Yom Tov a person is obligated to purify himself — they are more careful. That which is written in Teshuvat She'elah to a girl — according to Rashi and Rashbam z\"l, and Eliyah Rabbah explained: (1) the way of freedom — they dip before the meal to extend appetite for food. (2) They need to say the Haggadah and tell of the Exodus from Egypt; therefore they dip before eating without lingering, likewise without eating. (3) The children will ask about the second dipping: why — did they not already dip in the first? And the answer: dipping 1 is as all year, and dipping 2 is on account of the mitzvah of maror. And Taz explained that the question is on dipping 1 and the answer is to instruct about dipping 2; just as the first is not for the need of the meal, so the second only on account of the mitzvah of maror — a rabbinic mitzvah, for dipping is certainly only d'rabbanan, and maror too in our time — Magen Avraham and Eliyah Rabbah there will be explained. And see siman 475, see further in sign 8 of this:",
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger/part-001.txt|1:_": "<small>Magen Avraham seif kaf 2</small> until after the meal — nevertheless he is obligated to interrupt his meal and recite Havdalah, since he began in prohibition — see Magen Avraham siman 471 seif 12:",
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger/part-001.txt|2:_": "<small>Magen Avraham seif kaf 4</small> And I wonder why we are not accustomed [to take] from Yerushalmi chapter 9 of Shevi'it, brought in Tosafot chapter 9 mishnah 5 of Shabbat: what is karpas in the rivers of Bavel? Petroselinon:",
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger/part-001.txt|3:_": "<small>Magen Avraham seif kaf 8</small> That Yom Tov does not prepare for its fellow — it is not understood, for in any case on the night of the second Yom Tov too he will not eat it; and if he would eat it on the day of the second Yom Tov — only that generally he will not eat it on the first day of Yom Tov since he needs it for the night of the second Yom Tov; if so, what relevance is there in the introduction that one does not eat roasted and therefore it is forbidden? In any case, since he wishes to keep it for the second night and does not wish to eat it on the first, it is forbidden to roast it:",
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger/part-001.txt|4:_": "<small>Magen Avraham seif kaf 14</small> Everything standing to be uprooted is considered as uprooted — it does not suffice, for even now when it is fully cooked it is considered uprooted; nevertheless the gentile did not now acquire it and did not become a robber on it, as is stated in Bava Kamma 117b, brought in Magen Avraham below siman 637; and if so, when the Jew uproots he is the robber and there is no change of ownership:",
  "shaarei-teshuvah/part-001.txt|2:_": "<b>On Shabbat,</b> Avodat HaTodah and in Iggerot HaRamaz siman 13 he wrote, and this is its language: and regarding the matter of commerce and telling of the Exodus from Egypt — it is obvious to me that it is forbidden if the person is alone without guarding, end of his words — and it is astonishing, for it is against Shulchan Aruch siman 475; and it is possible it does not refer to telling the Haggadah but only that he wishes to tell, and it requires study:",
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1:_": "<b>One should not wash hands before Kiddush.</b> Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Mordechai: it is permitted to wash hands before Kiddush, for it is not diversion of attention; but on Pesach, where there is a great interruption in between, Beit Yosef should not wash hands before Kiddush, because he needs to wash hands another time to perform the dipping, and the first berachah will be in vain, etc.; and Beit Yosef wondered on this: what interruption is there more on Pesach than Shabbat and Yom Tov? And it appears there is a dispute, for it is stated in the chapter of Arvei Pesachim: if he washed hands at the first dipping, he washes hands at the second dipping. And they explain the reason: since he needs to say the Haggadah and Hallel, he diverted his attention and touched an unclean place. And Tosafot explained: this second dipping is not precise, for it is after HaMotzi; rather it refers to washing for the meal — for non-sacred [meat] does not require intention, it teaches us; and one must emend somewhat in Mordechai in that which he wrote \"because he needs,\" etc.; and it should read \"therefore he needs,\" and the reading is: one should not wash before Kiddush on account of the meal on Pesach; and he brings proof as they say that one must wash again for bread because he interrupted between the first washing with the Haggadah and Hallel; and if so, if he washes hands before Kiddush, all the more so the Haggadah and Hallel will be considered an interruption before the eating of matzah; and even without the Hagah one can explain thus, because in any case he needs to wash hands for bread — thus there is interruption here; and that which he mentioned to perform the dipping follows the language of the Gemara, and in the Gemara it is not precise as written in the name of Tosafot. And Rashal wrote in his order that one should wash hands before, so as not to change from other Kiddushim of the year; but nevertheless he should bring himself to obligation of washing by pouring on his feet and rubbing, so that he should be obligated to wash from before Kiddush and not bless al netilat yadayim but rather Asher Yatzar; and if so, for dipping he does not need washing, for this dipping is not better than actual eating — for all year Kiddush is not an interruption and he eats through washing before Kiddush, all the more so dipping; and Maharach z\"l required washing again for the first dipping, and I do not know the remedy in any matter for this extra washing before Kiddush on Pesach; for if on account of not changing from the rest of the year — behold he changes anyway, for now he does not bless al netilat yadayim and all year he blesses; and further, this remedy spoiled it, for when another sees this one washing he thinks he also blesses on it as the rest of the year, and in truth it is not fitting for him to bless now on Pesach because of the interruption as above; therefore the more correct [view] is as Rama wrote according to Mordechai, and it is the simple custom that they wash only until the first dipping. And that which Rashal wrote that one should not change from all year — I say: would that they would do so all year, to wash after Kiddush as written siman 471 seif 9:",
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt|2:_": "And he does not bless after it on the vine, etc., because he relies on what he will bless at the end of Grace after Meals — and see siman 474:",
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt|4:א": "<b>Or salt water.</b> And if it falls on Shabbat he should prepare the salt water before Shabbat and not make it on Shabbat, as written siman 321 seif 1; and if he did not make it before Shabbat and he has no vinegar, salt water should be made in small quantity, as written siman 321:",
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt|4:ב": "<b>And they are accustomed with meat and egg.</b> For meat they are accustomed to take a foreleg on account of \"outstretched arm,\" and it is a remembrance of the Pesach that was roasted on a spit; therefore they roast on coals. And an egg is a remembrance of the festival offering that they offered on the 14th and ate with the Pesach. And egg is the language of beya in Aramaic — meaning, the Merciful One seeks to redeem us. And Rashal wrote: the Ashkenaz custom to roast the meat and egg is because they do not eat them and keep them for the second night; and this custom is against the Talmud's custom — see chapter of Arvei Pesachim; and it is fitting to divide for each and they should be cooked, for it is impossible roasted; and I did not see in the Talmud from this that one must eat them; rather somewhat the opposite appears, for Rava said meat need not be raised; and not only that, but it appears as eating consecrated [meat] outside [the Temple]. Rashi explained: of the two cooked dishes — when he says \"the Pesach that our forefathers ate,\" he should not raise the meat that is a remembrance of the Pesach; behold it is forbidden to make raising with this meat, lest it appear as if he consecrated it while alive; and even though he says that our forefathers ate, it implies we have no Pesach — nevertheless it is forbidden to raise; if so, when he eats them and divides them and says remembrance of the Pesach, there is certainly more reason to be concerned for this; and further Rashbam and Tosafot wrote in the name of R' Chananel that the meat should be roasted — thus they do not eat; also from what Rama wrote that the egg they are accustomed to cook, the reason appears for this intention: the meat that they leave for remembrance they do not eat then, for it is only on account of remembrance of the Pesach; but the egg has another reason on account of mourning for the Temple, as Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Kol Bo; and if so they eat the egg in the manner of mourners in Bava Metzia. And they are accustomed to cook many eggs on that night for this reason, as Rama wrote siman 477; and therefore the egg should specifically be cooked, because they do not eat roasted on Pesach nights, as Rashal wrote as above — the custom of our forefathers is correct that they do not eat the meat, and the egg they eat; for without remembrance of the festival offering it has a reason on account of mourning, as written; and in siman 477 it will be explained that there is no prohibition of roasted on egg or fish — see there:",
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt|5:_": "<b>But not in their root.</b> Mahariu wrote: meaning the small roots that branch to here and to there; but the large root in which the vegetable stands is included in the stalk; thus I saw many people who at the time of eating maror take their vegetable, and at the time of wrapping take the stalk — and this has no reason or logic; for one should take the vegetable also at the time of wrapping, for he can eat well a k'zayit from it, for it does not distress as much as the stalk which has more strength:",
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt|6:א": "<b>He washes hands for the need of the first dipping.</b> For everything whose dipping is in liquid requires netilah. From here there is open rebuke to those who are not careful all year to wash before eating something whose dipping is in liquid — how is this night different from all the days of the year? And if he comes to purify himself more on this night, he should be careful at least on the ten days of repentance when everyone is careful to eat bread of a Jew specifically; but certainly here there is only lack of carefulness, and they do not scrutinize to equalize their measures:",
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt|6:ב": "<b>And he should not bless on the washing.</b> Tur wrote, and the Rambam wrote that nowadays one need not wash for something whose dipping is in liquid; therefore he would not bless al netilat yadayim on the first dipping — and likewise the author of Ittur, and I disagree; and likewise the Geonim that one must bless; and likewise Rosh in the name of Rashi; and one who wishes to fulfill doubt should bring himself to obligation of washing from another side, such as pouring water and rubbing. And the Rambam's reason: because washing is not on account of residue of terumah as with bread, but on account of cleanliness; and now we are not careful about cleanliness, as Tosafot wrote in the chapter of Arvei Pesachim; and Beit Yosef wrote that that which I disagree, his words are not correct, for since it is a dispute whether washing is required, it is better not to bless, end of his words. He further challenged that which Tur wrote \"pour water and rub\" — this thing does not bring him to berachah of al netilat yadayim according to the Rosh in siman 7; and I disagree: Tur did not write \"I disagree\" on the berachah but on that which he wrote there is no obligation of washing nowadays; also in that which he wrote \"one who wishes to fulfill doubt\" — it refers to washing, not to the berachah; for if we say washing is not required for this, there is prohibition to wash, for it is arrogance, like one who washes hands for fruits — he is among the arrogant because washing is not needed there, the same here; therefore he wrote that he should bring himself to washing through rubbing, and by necessity he is obligated in berachah of al netilat yadayim, since something whose dipping is in liquid has similarity to bread; thus one who poured water and rubbed and comes to eat this thing — all agree there is obligation of washing as with bread without rubbing; and in this all agree, since there are two factors elevating obligation of washing; and it is as one who wishes to learn in siman 7 — a pity, nevertheless Beit Yosef and Shulchan Aruch ruled well not to bless on this and not to heed that this berachah is written in all siddurim; and likewise Tosafot in the chapter of Arvei Pesachim page 115: one who blesses blesses a berachah in vain; and in all siddurim it is written to bless on washing — and it does not appear, end of his words. And one should greatly wonder that he wrote on this night because of the obligation of the festival one should bless, and all year he himself wrote in siman 158 that one should not bless — and this thing is not correct at all to obligate berachah at one time more than another; therefore one should not bless at all as all year. And the reason for this dipping — Rashi and Rashbam [explain] on account of recognition, because people are not accustomed to eat vegetable before the meal, and the children will ask about it; and the apparent answer will be for them: if he had dipped only the second of maror, it would not have been recognizable that it was on account of a mitzvah, but in the manner of people to dip during the meal; therefore they enacted to dip before the meal, for thereby they hint that the dipping does not come on account of the meal, and it is a sign for the second dipping, which is also not on account of the meal but a rabbinic mitzvah, as above according to Rashi and Rambam:",
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt|6:ג": "<b>Less than a k'zayit.</b> Because if he eats a k'zayit he will be obligated to bless Borei nafashot, and there is a dispute whether he is obligated to bless it on the first dipping; therefore it is better he eat in a manner where there is no obligation at all. And the dispute is between R' Yitzchak and Rashbam: R' Yitzchak holds that the Haggadah and Hallel are an interruption between the dipping and the meal, as is written regarding washing that one must wash again for the meal, as written in seif 1 — the same for blessing acharonah on the dipping, and the Grace after Meals of the meal does not exempt it; but Rashbam holds as the Rosh explained: only for washing is the Haggadah considered an interruption, lest he touched an unclean place in between; but for blessing acharonah, no — just as one need not bless Borei peri ha'adamah on maror because he is exempted by the berachah he made at the first dipping, so too for blessing acharonah of the first dipping he is exempt, for it is related to the meal and as if he ate during the meal. And R' Yitzchak holds: not on account of this reason is he exempt from Borei peri ha'adamah and blessing acharonah on maror, but maror itself is related to the meal and exempted by HaMotzi and Grace after Meals; but the first dipping has no relation to the meal because of the interruption of Haggadah and Hallel. And it appears that in this dispute there is also a dispute regarding blessing acharonah of the first cups: for R' Yitzchak one must bless, and for Rashbam not:",
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt|7:_": "<b>Why is this night different.</b> Tur wrote that one should not say \"we are not obligated to dip,\" etc., for it implies that on this night we are obligated to dip — and this is not so, for one is not obligated to dip except on account of recognition for children; rather he should say \"we do not dip,\" etc. And in our Gemara it states \"obligated to dip\" — and it requires study, for it is an error: <b>It is written</b> in Maharil that the large piece should be taken for afikoman and not less than half — meaning the small portion; and a reason can be given for this, because that half of afikoman is the essence of the mitzvah that he eats at the end: <b>Mordechai wrote</b> that one must say \"and we shall eat there of the sacrifices and of the Pesach offerings,\" for they eat first of the festival offering, which is the sacrifice; but the Pesach is eaten on the full [stomach]. And Mahariu wrote that if Pesach falls on Shabbat one must say that night \"of the Pesach offerings and of the sacrifices,\" for the festival offering is not offered on Shabbat. Tur wrote that one should say \"and we shall thank You, a new song,\" and not say \"a new shirah,\" since he speaks of the future redemption which is masculine language, as stated in the midrash, end of his words. And in the text in our siddurim it is written \"and we shall say before Him a new shirah, Halleluyah\" — this too is not correct, since he says \"v'ne'emar,\" it refers by necessity to the future redemption. And in some books they did not write at all this \"new shirah,\" and that is correct; for in truth it refers to the redemption that passed, for they recite the Egyptian Hallel. And in Mordechai of the elders of Mahari bar Betzalel z\"l it was vocalized \"v'ne'emar\" with segol, and this is more correct; and it is fitting to say \"a new shirah,\" for when \"ne'emar\" is with segol it is past tense:",
  "yad-ephraim/part-001.txt|1:_": "<b>473</b> in Taz seif kaf 1: he should wash hands at the second dipping. And he explains there reasons, etc.; and Tosafot explained this second dipping, etc.; and in truth it is not fitting for him to bless now on Pesach, for he will bless to wash again for bread — they do not fulfill with this washing because of the interruption, etc., so it should be:",
  "yad-ephraim/part-001.txt|2:_": "<b>(Taz seif kaf 4)</b> for it is impossible roasted, end of his words, and I did not see, etc. — thus they do not eat; also what the Mechaber wrote about the egg, etc., so it should be:",
  "yad-ephraim/part-001.txt|3:_": "<b>In Magen Avraham seif kaf 14</b> it is not established in the ground — it is like movable property, and gentiles acquire through abandonment, and there is change of ownership in the hand of the Jew, so it should be:",
  "yad-ephraim/part-001.txt|4:_": "<b>In Taz seif kaf 7</b> Tur wrote and R' Meir of Rothenburg wrote that nowadays, etc.; and the Rambam's reason because there is no washing, etc.; and one should greatly wonder on the Levush who wrote that on this night, etc., that if he had not dipped except the second of maror it would not have been recognizable, so it should be:",
};
